Object.assign(window.IND_FEED_BODY, {"sp-sci-janaki":{"route":"#/story/sci-janaki","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-janaki:place:0","title":"The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Kerala","Assam","Jharkhand"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Kerala."},"why":"A map question","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Kerala, Tamil Nadu"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Records of the Sugarcane Breeding Institute, Coimbatore; the Chromosome Atlas of Cultivated Plants (Darlington & Janaki Ammal, 1945); the Royal Horticultural Society, where Magnolia kobus 'Janaki Ammal' is recorded at Wisley; the Botanical Survey of India's institutional history; documented accounts of the Silent Valley campaign, and the park's establishment in 1984."]]},"sc-janaki_ammal":{"route":"#/avcard/janaki_ammal","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","cta":"Open their card","src":"avatar:janaki_ammal:achievements:0","title":"Janaki Ammal","body":"Is remembered as the first Indian woman to earn a doctorate in botany.","why":"Someone from The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Kerala, Tamil Nadu"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Records of the Sugarcane Breeding Institute, Coimbatore; the Chromosome Atlas of Cultivated Plants (Darlington & Janaki Ammal, 1945); the Royal Horticultural Society, where Magnolia kobus 'Janaki Ammal' is recorded at Wisley; the Botanical Survey of India's institutional history; documented accounts of the Silent Valley campaign, and the park's establishment in 1984."]]},"st-sci-bhabha":{"route":"#/story/sci-bhabha","art":"art/story/sci-bhabha.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-bhabha:scenes:0:text","title":"A Home for Science","body":"Homi Jehangir Bhabha, born in Bombay in 1909, was by his twenties a physicist with a growing name at Cambridge, working on cosmic rays — particles that fall on us from space. In 1939 he came home to India on holiday. Then the Second World War broke out, the sea routes closed, and the holiday quietly became the rest of his life.","why":"From Vigyan — The Scientists","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Bhabha's letter of 1944 to the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust, which survives in the archives of the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research and is loosely quoted here from that documented text; TIFR's own institutional history, including its founding in 1945 and its art collection."]]},"sh-sci-bhabha":{"route":"#/story/sci-bhabha","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-bhabha:hook","title":"A Home for Science","body":"A war trapped a young physicist in his own country. Instead of waiting for a ship back to England, he decided the science should move to India.","why":"From Vigyan — The Scientists","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Bhabha's letter of 1944 to the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust, which survives in the archives of the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research and is loosely quoted here from that documented text; TIFR's own institutional history, including its founding in 1945 and its art collection."]]},"sm-sci-bhabha":{"route":"#/story/sci-bhabha|s3","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","cta":"Read from this moment","src":"story:sci-bhabha:scenes:3:text","title":"A Home for Science — the middle","body":"The Trust said yes. The Tata Institute of Fundamental Research opened in 1945 and grew into its home by the sea in Bombay — and Bhabha, who was a genuinely fine painter himself, fussed over every detail of it: the gardens, the architecture, the modern paintings he hung on its walls, until the institute held one of India's great art collections. People asked why a physics institute needed beauty.","why":"A moment from A Home for Science","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Bhabha's letter of 1944 to the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust, which survives in the archives of the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research and is loosely quoted here from that documented text; TIFR's own institutional history, including its founding in 1945 and its art collection."]]},"sl-sci-bhabha":{"route":"#/story/sci-bhabha","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-bhabha:moral","title":"What A Home for Science says","body":"He did not wait for the future to arrive — he built the rooms it would need, and then it moved in.","why":"The story’s own lesson","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Bhabha's letter of 1944 to the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust, which survives in the archives of the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research and is loosely quoted here from that documented text; TIFR's own institutional history, including its founding in 1945 and its art collection."]]},"sp-sci-bhabha":{"route":"#/story/sci-bhabha","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-bhabha:place:0","title":"A Home for Science","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Maharashtra","Meghalaya","Tripura"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Maharashtra."},"why":"A map question","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Bhabha's letter of 1944 to the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust, which survives in the archives of the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research and is loosely quoted here from that documented text; TIFR's own institutional history, including its founding in 1945 and its art collection."]]},"sc-bhabha":{"route":"#/avcard/bhabha","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","cta":"Open their card","src":"avatar:bhabha:achievements:0","title":"Homi Bhabha","body":"Worked on cosmic rays at Cambridge, then came home for a holiday that a war turned into the rest of his life.","why":"Someone from A Home for Science","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Bhabha's letter of 1944 to the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust, which survives in the archives of the Tata Institute of Fundamental Research and is loosely quoted here from that documented text; TIFR's own institutional history, including its founding in 1945 and its art collection."]]},"st-sci-swaminathan":{"route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan","art":"art/story/sci-swaminathan.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-swaminathan:scenes:0:text","title":"The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","body":"Monkombu Sambasivan Swaminathan, born in Kumbakonam in Tamil Nadu in 1925, was a student in 1943 when famine struck Bengal and people died in the streets in numbers that are hard to write down. He said ever afterwards that it was this that decided him: he had been headed for medicine, and he turned instead to the science of growing food. He wanted, he said, a hungry-proof India.","why":"From Vigyan — The Scientists","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Tamil Nadu"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","M. S. Swaminathan's own accounts of 1943 and the wheat years, as recorded by the M. S. Swaminathan Research Foundation; documented histories of the 1968 harvest, including the schools-as-granaries detail; \"evergreen revolution\" is his own published phrase; World Food Prize records, 1987."]]},"sh-sci-swaminathan":{"route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-swaminathan:hook","title":"The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","body":"India was living ship-to-mouth, waiting on grain boats. The fix turned out to hinge on something absurdly simple: the height of a wheat stem.","why":"From Vigyan — The Scientists","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Tamil Nadu"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","M. S. Swaminathan's own accounts of 1943 and the wheat years, as recorded by the M. S. Swaminathan Research Foundation; documented histories of the 1968 harvest, including the schools-as-granaries detail; \"evergreen revolution\" is his own published phrase; World Food Prize records, 1987."]]},"sm-sci-swaminathan":{"route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan|s2","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","cta":"Read from this moment","src":"story:sci-swaminathan:scenes:2:text","title":"The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over — the middle","body":"Swaminathan and his colleagues brought in the dwarf wheats bred by Norman Borlaug in Mexico — he invited Borlaug to India in 1963 — and crossed and trialled them for Indian fields and Indian rotis. Farmers, above all in Punjab, took the new seed and ran with it.","why":"A moment from The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Tamil Nadu"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","M. S. Swaminathan's own accounts of 1943 and the wheat years, as recorded by the M. S. Swaminathan Research Foundation; documented histories of the 1968 harvest, including the schools-as-granaries detail; \"evergreen revolution\" is his own published phrase; World Food Prize records, 1987."]]},"sl-sci-swaminathan":{"route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-swaminathan:moral","title":"What The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over says","body":"He fed a country and then kept asking whether he had done it right — the second thing is as rare as the first.","why":"The story’s own lesson","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Tamil Nadu"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","M. S. Swaminathan's own accounts of 1943 and the wheat years, as recorded by the M. S. Swaminathan Research Foundation; documented histories of the 1968 harvest, including the schools-as-granaries detail; \"evergreen revolution\" is his own published phrase; World Food Prize records, 1987."]]},"sp-sci-swaminathan":{"route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-swaminathan:place:0","title":"The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Tamil Nadu","Uttar Pradesh","Goa"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Tamil Nadu."},"why":"A map question","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Tamil Nadu"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","M. S. Swaminathan's own accounts of 1943 and the wheat years, as recorded by the M. S. Swaminathan Research Foundation; documented histories of the 1968 harvest, including the schools-as-granaries detail; \"evergreen revolution\" is his own published phrase; World Food Prize records, 1987."]]},"sc-swaminathan":{"route":"#/avcard/swaminathan","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","cta":"Open their card","src":"avatar:swaminathan:achievements:0","title":"M. S. Swaminathan","body":"Turned from medicine to the science of growing food after watching the Bengal famine of 1943.","why":"Someone from The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Tamil Nadu"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","M. S. Swaminathan's own accounts of 1943 and the wheat years, as recorded by the M. S. Swaminathan Research Foundation; documented histories of the 1968 harvest, including the schools-as-granaries detail; \"evergreen revolution\" is his own published phrase; World Food Prize records, 1987."]]},"st-sci-salimali":{"route":"#/story/sci-salimali","art":"art/story/sci-salimali.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-salimali:scenes:0:text","title":"The Fall of a Sparrow","body":"Salim Ali grew up in Bombay around 1900, an orphan raised in a big, warm houseful of uncles and cousins, and like a lot of boys then he had an airgun and used it on sparrows. One day he picked up a sparrow he had shot and stopped. On its throat was a patch of yellow. Sparrows, every boy knew, do not have yellow throats. So what exactly was lying in his hand?","why":"From Vigyan — The Scientists","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Salim Ali's own memoir, The Fall of a Sparrow (1985) — the yellow-throated sparrow, Mr Millard and the drawers of bird skins are his own telling; the Bombay Natural History Society's records of his surveys; Keoladeo National Park's documented history."]]},"sh-sci-salimali":{"route":"#/story/sci-salimali","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-salimali:hook","title":"The Fall of a Sparrow","body":"A ten-year-old with an airgun shot a sparrow — and the question he asked next turned him into the Birdman of India. He told the story himself.","why":"From Vigyan — The Scientists","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Salim Ali's own memoir, The Fall of a Sparrow (1985) — the yellow-throated sparrow, Mr Millard and the drawers of bird skins are his own telling; the Bombay Natural History Society's records of his surveys; Keoladeo National Park's documented history."]]},"sm-sci-salimali":{"route":"#/story/sci-salimali|s3","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","cta":"Read from this moment","src":"story:sci-salimali:scenes:3:text","title":"The Fall of a Sparrow — the middle","body":"And because he knew where the birds lived, he knew what they could not live without. When the wetland at Bharatpur — a city of nesting storks, herons and cranes, and winter home to birds from across the world — needed defending, his voice was one of the loudest raised for it; he fought similar corners all his life, Silent Valley's rainforest among them.","why":"A moment from The Fall of a Sparrow","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Salim Ali's own memoir, The Fall of a Sparrow (1985) — the yellow-throated sparrow, Mr Millard and the drawers of bird skins are his own telling; the Bombay Natural History Society's records of his surveys; Keoladeo National Park's documented history."]]},"sl-sci-salimali":{"route":"#/story/sci-salimali","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-salimali:moral","title":"What The Fall of a Sparrow says","body":"He was not the boy who never did wrong — he was the boy who looked closely at what he had done, and asked.","why":"The story’s own lesson","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Salim Ali's own memoir, The Fall of a Sparrow (1985) — the yellow-throated sparrow, Mr Millard and the drawers of bird skins are his own telling; the Bombay Natural History Society's records of his surveys; Keoladeo National Park's documented history."]]},"sp-sci-salimali":{"route":"#/story/sci-salimali","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-salimali:place:0","title":"The Fall of a Sparrow","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Maharashtra","Chandigarh","Kerala"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Maharashtra."},"why":"A map question","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Salim Ali's own memoir, The Fall of a Sparrow (1985) — the yellow-throated sparrow, Mr Millard and the drawers of bird skins are his own telling; the Bombay Natural History Society's records of his surveys; Keoladeo National Park's documented history."]]},"sc-salimali":{"route":"#/avcard/salimali","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","cta":"Open their card","src":"avatar:salimali:achievements:0","title":"Salim Ali","body":"Surveyed India’s birds state by state for decades, with notebooks, patience and famously little money.","why":"Someone from The Fall of a Sparrow","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Maharashtra"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","Salim Ali's own memoir, The Fall of a Sparrow (1985) — the yellow-throated sparrow, Mr Millard and the drawers of bird skins are his own telling; the Bombay Natural History Society's records of his surveys; Keoladeo National Park's documented history."]]},"st-sci-annamani":{"route":"#/story/sci-annamani","art":"art/story/sci-annamani.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-annamani:scenes:0:text","title":"The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","body":"Anna Mani was born in 1918 in Peermade, in the hills of Kerala, into a prosperous family with settled ideas about what girls were for. By eight she had read most of what her town could offer a child. Then came her eighth birthday, and with it the family custom: a gift of diamond earrings.","why":"From Vigyan — The Scientists","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Kerala"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","The World Meteorological Organization's published tribute to Anna Mani; her documented interviews, retold in Abha Sur's \"Dispersed Radiance\", which carry the encyclopaedia-instead-of-earrings story in her own telling, and the Raman-laboratory years with the withheld doctorate; India Meteorological Department institutional history for the standardisation of some hundred weather instruments, the ozone work and International Ozone Commission membership, and the solar radiation and wind-energy handbooks and survey stations of her later years."]]},"sh-sci-annamani":{"route":"#/story/sci-annamani","art":"art/story/sm/sci-annamani.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-annamani:hook","title":"The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","body":"On her eighth birthday, her family offered the customary gift of diamond earrings. She had a different request — and it ran her whole life.","why":"From Vigyan — The Scientists","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Kerala"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","The World Meteorological Organization's published tribute to Anna Mani; her documented interviews, retold in Abha Sur's \"Dispersed Radiance\", which carry the encyclopaedia-instead-of-earrings story in her own telling, and the Raman-laboratory years with the withheld doctorate; India Meteorological Department institutional history for the standardisation of some hundred weather instruments, the ozone work and International Ozone Commission membership, and the solar radiation and wind-energy handbooks and survey stations of her later years."]]},"sm-sci-annamani":{"route":"#/story/sci-annamani|s3","art":"art/story/sm/sci-annamani.jpg","cta":"Read from this moment","src":"story:sci-annamani:scenes:3:text","title":"The Girl Who Refused the Earrings — the middle","body":"Anna Mani made that her life's work. She gathered and standardised the drawings of about a hundred weather instruments and drove Indian workshops to manufacture them to her exacting standards — she was famously exacting — until the weather service of India ran on instruments made in India, and the importing stopped.","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Kerala"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","The World Meteorological Organization's published tribute to Anna Mani; her documented interviews, retold in Abha Sur's \"Dispersed Radiance\", which carry the encyclopaedia-instead-of-earrings story in her own telling, and the Raman-laboratory years with the withheld doctorate; India Meteorological Department institutional history for the standardisation of some hundred weather instruments, the ozone work and International Ozone Commission membership, and the solar radiation and wind-energy handbooks and survey stations of her later years."]]},"sl-sci-annamani":{"route":"#/story/sci-annamani","art":"art/story/sm/sci-annamani.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-annamani:moral","title":"What The Girl Who Refused the Earrings says","body":"She spent her life measuring what cannot be seen — wind, ozone, the weight of air — and her trick was always the same: build the instrument yourself.","why":"The story’s own lesson","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Kerala"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","The World Meteorological Organization's published tribute to Anna Mani; her documented interviews, retold in Abha Sur's \"Dispersed Radiance\", which carry the encyclopaedia-instead-of-earrings story in her own telling, and the Raman-laboratory years with the withheld doctorate; India Meteorological Department institutional history for the standardisation of some hundred weather instruments, the ozone work and International Ozone Commission membership, and the solar radiation and wind-energy handbooks and survey stations of her later years."]]},"sw-sci-annamani-2":{"route":"#/story/sci-annamani","art":"art/story/sm/sci-annamani.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-annamani:words_hi:2","title":"A Hindi word from The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","text":"जिज्ञासा","roman":"jigyasa","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["curiosity","sprout","bee"],"a":0,"after":"जिज्ञासा (jigyasa) means “curiosity”."},"why":"A word from The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Kerala"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","The World Meteorological Organization's published tribute to Anna Mani; her documented interviews, retold in Abha Sur's \"Dispersed Radiance\", which carry the encyclopaedia-instead-of-earrings story in her own telling, and the Raman-laboratory years with the withheld doctorate; India Meteorological Department institutional history for the standardisation of some hundred weather instruments, the ozone work and International Ozone Commission membership, and the solar radiation and wind-energy handbooks and survey stations of her later years."]]},"sp-sci-annamani":{"route":"#/story/sci-annamani","art":"art/story/sm/sci-annamani.jpg","cta":"Read the story","src":"story:sci-annamani:place:0","title":"The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","play":{"q":"Which place on the map does this story light up?","opts":["Kerala","Maharashtra","Tamil Nadu"],"a":0,"after":"It lights up Kerala."},"why":"A map question","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Kerala"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","The World Meteorological Organization's published tribute to Anna Mani; her documented interviews, retold in Abha Sur's \"Dispersed Radiance\", which carry the encyclopaedia-instead-of-earrings story in her own telling, and the Raman-laboratory years with the withheld doctorate; India Meteorological Department institutional history for the standardisation of some hundred weather instruments, the ozone work and International Ozone Commission membership, and the solar radiation and wind-energy handbooks and survey stations of her later years."]]},"sc-annamani":{"route":"#/avcard/annamani","art":"art/story/sm/sci-annamani.jpg","cta":"Open their card","src":"avatar:annamani:achievements:0","title":"Anna Mani","body":"Wrote five research papers in C. V. Raman’s laboratory — and was never granted the doctorate they had earned.","why":"Someone from The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Lights up","Kerala"],["Read","4 min"],["Told from","The World Meteorological Organization's published tribute to Anna Mani; her documented interviews, retold in Abha Sur's \"Dispersed Radiance\", which carry the encyclopaedia-instead-of-earrings story in her own telling, and the Raman-laboratory years with the withheld doctorate; India Meteorological Department institutional history for the standardisation of some hundred weather instruments, the ozone work and International Ozone Commission membership, and the solar radiation and wind-energy handbooks and survey stations of her later years."]]},"ep-mahabharata-26":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-26-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:0:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"The chariot stopped in the middle, between the two lines, with everybody watching. And Arjuna, who had wanted this war for thirteen years, stood up and looked along the other side properly for the first time.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"],["Hear it","6 min"]]},"eh-mahabharata-26":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-26-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:hook","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"He put the bow down between two armies and said he could not do it.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"],["Hear it","6 min"]]},"em-mahabharata-26":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-26-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:6:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends — the middle","body":"Krishna did not shout. He asked him questions. He started by pointing out the thing Arjuna had not noticed — that sitting down in the chariot is not stepping out of the world. It is a choice, with a price, and other people pay it too. There is no square on that field marked innocent.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"],["Hear it","6 min"]]},"ew-mahabharata-26":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-26-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:wonder","title":"Something to wonder about","body":"Krishna told Arjuna that sitting down in the chariot was also a choice, and that other people would pay for it. Is there anywhere in something like that where a person can stand and not be part of it?","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"],["Hear it","6 min"]]},"ewd-mahabharata-26-0":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-26-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:words_hi:0","title":"A Hindi word from A Talk Between Friends","text":"मन","roman":"man","lang":"hi","play":{"q":"What does it mean?","opts":["mind","equal","protection"],"a":0,"after":"मन (man) means “mind”."},"why":"A word from A Talk Between Friends","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"],["Hear it","6 min"]]},"ep-mahabharata-33":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-33-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:0:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"They ruled for a long time, and it was fine, and it was quiet, and nobody in the palace ever quite got the field out of their head. And then one morning they took off the crowns, left them on a chair, and walked out of the city going north, with Draupadi, on foot.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"],["Hear it","5 min"]]},"eh-mahabharata-33":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-33-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:hook","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"Everything he wanted was on the other side of the gate. He would not go in.","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"],["Hear it","5 min"]]},"em-mahabharata-33":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-33-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate — the middle","body":"\"Then it is not a place I want,\" said Yudhishthira. \"That animal has walked every step I have walked. Nobody asked it to. It stayed when my wife did not last, and my brothers did not last. I am not going to buy a seat in heaven by leaving something on a mountain because it followed me.\"","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"],["Hear it","5 min"]]},"ew-mahabharata-33":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"art":"art/epic/sm/mahabharata-33-0.jpg","cta":"Hear this night","src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:wonder","title":"Something to wonder about","body":"Yudhishthira gave up everything he had walked all that way for rather than leave behind an animal that had followed him and was never asked to. What do you owe something that chose to come with you?","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"],["Hear it","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.sandrembi-1":{"src":"story:fk.sandrembi:scenes:1:text","title":"Sandrembi and the Grey Dove · 2 of 11","body":"Sandrembi's own mother was gone. But in the pond behind the house there was a tortoise who had not been there before — and who knew Sandrembi's name, and her mother's voice, and everything a mother knows. Sandrembi went to the pond every evening and told her the whole day.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sandrembi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sandrembi|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Sandrembi and the Grey Dove","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.sandrembi-2":{"src":"story:fk.sandrembi:scenes:2:text","title":"Sandrembi and the Grey Dove · 3 of 11","body":"One morning the king of that country came by the water while the girls were out with their fishing baskets. Chaisra pushed to the front at once. Sandrembi did not notice him at all — she was busy giving half her catch to an old woman who had caught nothing, which was a thing she simply did.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sandrembi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sandrembi|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Sandrembi and the Grey Dove","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.sandrembi-3":{"src":"story:fk.sandrembi:scenes:3:text","title":"Sandrembi and the Grey Dove · 4 of 11","body":"The king noticed. Kings notice more than they let on. And when he asked for Sandrembi, she went first to the pond. \"Go,\" said the tortoise from the water. \"And when you are a queen, stay the girl who shared the fish. That is all I have ever wanted of you.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sandrembi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sandrembi|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Sandrembi and the Grey Dove","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.sandrembi-4":{"src":"story:fk.sandrembi:scenes:4:text","title":"Sandrembi and the Grey Dove · 5 of 11","body":"Sandrembi was a good queen, and Chaisra's mother could not bear it. Jealousy did what jealousy does: it found a way. One morning the queen was simply gone, and Chaisra sat in her place wearing her clothes — and over the palace a grey dove flew in circles and would not leave.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sandrembi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sandrembi|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Sandrembi and the Grey Dove","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.sandrembi-6":{"src":"story:fk.sandrembi:scenes:6:text","title":"Sandrembi and the Grey Dove · 7 of 11","body":"And every day the grey dove came to his window, and sang, and would not be shooed.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sandrembi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sandrembi|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Sandrembi and the Grey Dove","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.sandrembi-7":{"src":"story:fk.sandrembi:scenes:7:text","title":"Sandrembi and the Grey Dove · 8 of 11","body":"It was the pond song. The one his queen used to hum with her hands in the rice. He held out his wrist and the dove came down onto it, light as a held breath, and looked at him with an eye he knew.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sandrembi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sandrembi|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Sandrembi and the Grey Dove","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.sandrembi-8":{"src":"story:fk.sandrembi:scenes:8:text","title":"Sandrembi and the Grey Dove · 9 of 11","body":"He carried the dove to the pond behind her old house. The tortoise rose in the dark water and the dove came down to meet her, and between the mother in the water and the love on the bank, the shape came right again — and Sandrembi stood on the grass in the morning sun.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sandrembi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sandrembi|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Sandrembi and the Grey Dove","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.sandrembi-9":{"src":"story:fk.sandrembi:scenes:9:text","title":"Sandrembi and the Grey Dove · 10 of 11","body":"Chaisra and her mother went home. The old tellings say much more about what happened to them, and it is harsh, and I do not tell it that way. They went home. That is enough.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sandrembi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sandrembi|s9","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Sandrembi and the Grey Dove","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.khamba-thoibi-1":{"src":"story:fk.khamba-thoibi:scenes:1:text","title":"Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull · 2 of 11","body":"Khamba had no father and no mother. He had one sister, Khamnu, who raised him on next to nothing and absolutely no nonsense, and he grew up poor, polite, and strong enough to carry a boat on his back — which in Moirang people noticed.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khamba-thoibi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khamba-thoibi|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.khamba-thoibi-2":{"src":"story:fk.khamba-thoibi:scenes:2:text","title":"Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull · 3 of 11","body":"Thoibi was the princess of Moirang, and she met Khamba at the lake, the way half the stories in Manipur begin. They talked about nothing much. Then they talked about everything. That part of the story takes the pena singers a whole evening, and they do not hurry it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khamba-thoibi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khamba-thoibi|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.khamba-thoibi-3":{"src":"story:fk.khamba-thoibi:scenes:3:text","title":"Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull · 4 of 11","body":"But another man wanted to marry Thoibi — Nongban, who was rich, and used to winning. So it came to a contest, because Moirang settled things with contests, and Moirang took its sport seriously: you won, you lost, you bowed, and you ate together after.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khamba-thoibi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khamba-thoibi|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.khamba-thoibi-4":{"src":"story:fk.khamba-thoibi:scenes:4:text","title":"Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull · 5 of 11","body":"At the festival of Thangjing they wrestled. Drums, dust, the whole town on its feet. Nongban was strong and knew it. Khamba was strong and stood very still, and waited, and when the moment came he took the grip, turned his hips, and set Nongban down on the grass like a full water pot — firmly, and spilling nothing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khamba-thoibi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khamba-thoibi|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.khamba-thoibi-6":{"src":"story:fk.khamba-thoibi:scenes:6:text","title":"Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull · 7 of 11","body":"Khamba stood at the edge of the trees and looked at the bull, and the bull turned and looked at him. And Khamba was carrying something up that hill that nobody else had ever brought.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khamba-thoibi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khamba-thoibi|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.khamba-thoibi-7":{"src":"story:fk.khamba-thoibi:scenes:7:text","title":"Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull · 8 of 11","body":"He walked out slow, hands open, no rope showing. And he spoke his father's name, and the old calling words his sister had taught him, the ones the bull had heard every morning as a calf. The great ears turned. The great head came down. Kao stood still, remembering.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khamba-thoibi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khamba-thoibi|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.khamba-thoibi-8":{"src":"story:fk.khamba-thoibi:scenes:8:text","title":"Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull · 9 of 11","body":"Khamba led him down into Moirang on a slack rope, walking beside him like an old friend, and the town was so quiet you could hear the lake — and then so loud you could not hear anything at all.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khamba-thoibi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khamba-thoibi|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.khamba-thoibi-9":{"src":"story:fk.khamba-thoibi:scenes:9:text","title":"Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull · 10 of 11","body":"Khamba and Thoibi were married in front of the whole of Moirang, with Thoibi in the finest phanek ever woven, and a feast that fed everybody including Nongban. The pena singers were composing before the rice was served.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khamba-thoibi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khamba-thoibi|s9","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Khamba, Thoibi and the Great Bull","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.loktak-ima-1":{"src":"story:fk.loktak-ima:scenes:1:text","title":"The Lake That Holds Everything Up · 2 of 9","body":"A girl called Sana went out by boat to stay with her grandmother, whose hut stood on one of them — a floating hut on a floating meadow, with a floating vegetable patch by the door. Her grandmother's whole house was somewhere slightly different every morning.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-loktak-ima.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.loktak-ima|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lake That Holds Everything Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.loktak-ima-2":{"src":"story:fk.loktak-ima:scenes:2:text","title":"The Lake That Holds Everything Up · 3 of 9","body":"\"Abok,\" said Sana — grandmother — \"why do the islands float? Islands are supposed to stay down.\" Her grandmother went on mending her net. \"Because of who the lake is,\" she said. \"We call her Ima Loktak. Ima means mother. A mother holds up what she loves.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-loktak-ima.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.loktak-ima|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lake That Holds Everything Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.loktak-ima-3":{"src":"story:fk.loktak-ima:scenes:3:text","title":"The Lake That Holds Everything Up · 4 of 9","body":"Sana thought about that all day, while the fishers lifted their nets out of water the colour of tea, and the mist walked about between the huts, and the whole village rose and fell very gently, like something breathing.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-loktak-ima.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.loktak-ima|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lake That Holds Everything Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.loktak-ima-5":{"src":"story:fk.loktak-ima:scenes:5:text","title":"The Lake That Holds Everything Up · 6 of 9","body":"The sangai is a deer with antlers like swept branches, found nowhere else in the world but here. And it does not walk the way other deer walk. It steps high and soft and careful, lifting each foot like a dancer — which is why Manipur calls it the dancing deer.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-loktak-ima.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.loktak-ima|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lake That Holds Everything Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.loktak-ima-6":{"src":"story:fk.loktak-ima:scenes:6:text","title":"The Lake That Holds Everything Up · 7 of 9","body":"\"The old people say the sangai is the tie between us and the land,\" said her grandmother. \"Harm the deer, and you have cut something in yourself. There was a time when so few were left you could count them like family members. Manipur guarded them back, one by one.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-loktak-ima.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.loktak-ima|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lake That Holds Everything Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.loktak-ima-7":{"src":"story:fk.loktak-ima:scenes:7:text","title":"The Lake That Holds Everything Up · 8 of 9","body":"They watched a stag pick his way across the meadow at dusk, the ground swaying under him no more than a boat sways, his feet reading it the whole time. He never once looked down.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-loktak-ima.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.loktak-ima|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lake That Holds Everything Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sagol-kangjei-1":{"src":"story:fk.sagol-kangjei:scenes:1:text","title":"The Game That Rode Out of Manipur · 2 of 9","body":"The Meitei say the game did not begin with people at all. It began with the gods. Marjing, the god of horses, keeper of a winged pony called Samadon Ayangba, brought the game — and the old accounts say the very first match was played by gods, seven a side, before anyone on earth had thought of it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sagol-kangjei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sagol-kangjei|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Game That Rode Out of Manipur","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sagol-kangjei-2":{"src":"story:fk.sagol-kangjei:scenes:2:text","title":"The Game That Rode Out of Manipur · 3 of 9","body":"Then people played it, on the Manipuri pony — a small, quick, sturdy creature that can turn on a leaf. The pony is honoured in Manipur, and not as a beast of burden. It is not for the plough and not for the cart. It is for the game.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sagol-kangjei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sagol-kangjei|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Game That Rode Out of Manipur","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sagol-kangjei-3":{"src":"story:fk.sagol-kangjei:scenes:3:text","title":"The Game That Rode Out of Manipur · 4 of 9","body":"Seven a side. A ball cut from bamboo root. Cane sticks, a flying gallop, and no argument about whose game it was, because it was everybody's — kings played commoners on that ground, and the pony did not care which was which.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sagol-kangjei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sagol-kangjei|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Game That Rode Out of Manipur","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sagol-kangjei-5":{"src":"story:fk.sagol-kangjei:scenes:5:text","title":"The Game That Rode Out of Manipur · 6 of 9","body":"The visitors asked to be taught. And some people in the town said what people always say: it is ours. Keep it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sagol-kangjei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sagol-kangjei|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Game That Rode Out of Manipur","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sagol-kangjei-6":{"src":"story:fk.sagol-kangjei:scenes:6:text","title":"The Game That Rode Out of Manipur · 7 of 9","body":"The visitors carried it off and called it polo. They made clubs — first near these hills, then in Calcutta, then in England — and from England it spread to every continent there is. Every polo field on earth is a grandchild of that grass in Imphal.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sagol-kangjei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sagol-kangjei|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Game That Rode Out of Manipur","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sagol-kangjei-7":{"src":"story:fk.sagol-kangjei:scenes:7:text","title":"The Game That Rode Out of Manipur · 8 of 9","body":"And on Mapal Kangjeibung they still play it the old way, seven a side, on Manipuri ponies. Above Imphal, on Marjing's hill, stands a great statue of a winged pony, looking out over the valley where it all started.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sagol-kangjei.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sagol-kangjei|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Game That Rode Out of Manipur","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tapta-1":{"src":"story:fk.tapta:scenes:1:text","title":"Tapta · 2 of 10","body":"A tiger came down out of the hills that night to see what he could find, and stopped under the eaves of the hut to get out of the wet. Through the wall he could hear the old woman inside, talking to herself, the way people who live alone do.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tapta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tapta|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tapta","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tapta-2":{"src":"story:fk.tapta:scenes:2:text","title":"Tapta · 3 of 10","body":"\"Tiger?\" she was saying. \"I am not afraid of tiger. Bear? Pff. Elephant? Let him come. It is TAPTA I cannot stand. Tapta gets in everywhere. Tapta finds you wherever you sit. Move the bed and Tapta follows you. Ah — here it comes again — TAPTA!\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tapta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tapta|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tapta","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tapta-3":{"src":"story:fk.tapta:scenes:3:text","title":"Tapta · 4 of 10","body":"The tiger went very cold, and it was not the rain. Something that outranked tiger, bear AND elephant. Something that got in everywhere and found you wherever you sat. And it was in the house with her, right now.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tapta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tapta|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tapta","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tapta-4":{"src":"story:fk.tapta:scenes:4:text","title":"Tapta · 5 of 10","body":"Now, that same black wet night, a thief came creeping to the old woman's lean-to, meaning to steal her cow. It was far too dark to see. He felt about, found something big and warm and furry, and — being a thief in a hurry — climbed straight up onto its back to ride it away.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tapta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tapta|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tapta","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tapta-6":{"src":"story:fk.tapta:scenes:6:text","title":"Tapta · 7 of 10","body":"And so they went through the night, flat out: the thief hanging on with both hands thinking, this is the fastest cow in Manipur — and the tiger flying over ditches thinking, Tapta rides me, Tapta rides me, and if I stop it will eat me. Neither one of them dared let go.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tapta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tapta|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tapta","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tapta-7":{"src":"story:fk.tapta:scenes:7:text","title":"Tapta · 8 of 10","body":"At first light the thief looked down and saw stripes. He made a sound no one has spelled correctly to this day, went straight up into a fig tree, and stayed there until noon. The tiger, feeling the terrible weight leave his back, ran faster — and some say he is running still.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tapta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tapta|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tapta","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tapta-8":{"src":"story:fk.tapta:scenes:8:text","title":"Tapta · 9 of 10","body":"The old woman woke to a quiet, rinsed morning. Her cow was exactly where it should be. She never knew she had been guarded all night by the most frightening word in the hills — which was hers.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tapta.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tapta|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Tapta","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.phunga-wari-1":{"src":"story:fk.phunga-wari:scenes:1:text","title":"The Fire Where the Stories Live · 2 of 8","body":"In the cold month the Meitei calendar calls Wakching, the evenings turn long and the valley fog comes down, and that is the season the phunga earns its keep. Dinner done, the children fold themselves in close, smallest nearest the warmth.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-phunga-wari.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.phunga-wari|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Fire Where the Stories Live","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.phunga-wari-2":{"src":"story:fk.phunga-wari:scenes:2:text","title":"The Fire Where the Stories Live · 3 of 8","body":"Grandmother had rules, and the rules were old. The fire is fed before anybody is told anything. Stories come after dinner, \"because a story on an empty stomach gets interrupted.\" And nobody asks how a story ends. It ends when it ends.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-phunga-wari.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.phunga-wari|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Fire Where the Stories Live","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.phunga-wari-3":{"src":"story:fk.phunga-wari:scenes:3:text","title":"The Fire Where the Stories Live · 4 of 8","body":"And the hearth itself is not furniture. Every winter, at Emoinu Eratpa, the best of the kitchen is set out for Emoinu, the kindly goddess of the hearth and of plenty — because in a Meitei home, somebody lives at the fire, in the way that matters.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-phunga-wari.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.phunga-wari|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Fire Where the Stories Live","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.phunga-wari-5":{"src":"story:fk.phunga-wari:scenes:5:text","title":"The Fire Where the Stories Live · 6 of 8","body":"So the wood went in, and the flames sat up, and grandmother began — Pebet tonight, because the littlest liked the part with the thorn bush. Halfway through, he was asleep against his sister's arm, and was carried to bed still holding a fistful of her sleeve.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-phunga-wari.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.phunga-wari|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Fire Where the Stories Live","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.phunga-wari-6":{"src":"story:fk.phunga-wari:scenes:6:text","title":"The Fire Where the Stories Live · 7 of 8","body":"\"The story waits,\" said grandmother, banking the fire. \"It stops exactly where it stopped, until tomorrow.\" She had learned it at a phunga herself, from her own grandmother, who had learned it at another one. A story is a fire that is fed by being given away.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-phunga-wari.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.phunga-wari|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Fire Where the Stories Live","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","3 min"]]},"ss-fk.lai-haraoba-1":{"src":"story:fk.lai-haraoba:scenes:1:text","title":"The Dance That Remembers the Making · 2 of 9","body":"A girl called Ibemma went with her grandfather. The pena players were playing — the little fiddle with the long memory — and the maibis, the priestesses, were dancing in a slow line, and their hands were making small, careful, exact shapes, over and over.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lai-haraoba.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lai-haraoba|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Dance That Remembers the Making","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lai-haraoba-2":{"src":"story:fk.lai-haraoba:scenes:2:text","title":"The Dance That Remembers the Making · 3 of 9","body":"\"Watch the hands,\" her grandfather whispered. \"Now they are making the body — see, the head, then the ribs, then the joints, one by one. Now the house — the posts going up, the beams across, the thatch coming down over it.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lai-haraoba.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lai-haraoba|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Dance That Remembers the Making","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lai-haraoba-3":{"src":"story:fk.lai-haraoba:scenes:3:text","title":"The Dance That Remembers the Making · 4 of 9","body":"The hands went on. They planted cotton. They picked it. They spun it into thread, and the thread into cloth, and the cloth went — in one lovely last gesture — around a person's shoulders. The whole of getting dressed, from seed to shawl, inside one dance.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lai-haraoba.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lai-haraoba|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Dance That Remembers the Making","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lai-haraoba-5":{"src":"story:fk.lai-haraoba:scenes:5:text","title":"The Dance That Remembers the Making · 6 of 9","body":"\"Writing things down is one way of remembering,\" said her grandfather. \"This is another. Older. Nobody has to be able to read it — you only have to keep dancing it, and nothing is lost.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lai-haraoba.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lai-haraoba|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Dance That Remembers the Making","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lai-haraoba-6":{"src":"story:fk.lai-haraoba:scenes:6:text","title":"The Dance That Remembers the Making · 7 of 9","body":"The whole neighbourhood was there — the aunties with flasks of tea, the boys pretending not to be interested and watching anyway, the god invited down to enjoy it all, because that is what the festival is: the gods, being given a good time by their own people.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lai-haraoba.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lai-haraoba|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Dance That Remembers the Making","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.lai-haraoba-7":{"src":"story:fk.lai-haraoba:scenes:7:text","title":"The Dance That Remembers the Making · 8 of 9","body":"On the walk home her grandfather said: \"When you plant something, or weave something, or build a roof — you are doing the holy things slowly. The maibis do them beautifully, once a year, so that everyone remembers what the ordinary work is.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-lai-haraoba.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.lai-haraoba|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Dance That Remembers the Making","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ao-six-stones-1":{"src":"story:fk.ao-six-stones:scenes:1:text","title":"The Six Stones and the River · 2 of 9","body":"Out of the earth at those stones, the telling goes, came the first ancestors — three men and three women, up into the daylight of the hills. Not from somewhere else. From here. The Ao are a people whose beginning has an address.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ao-six-stones.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ao-six-stones|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Six Stones and the River","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ao-six-stones-2":{"src":"story:fk.ao-six-stones:scenes:2:text","title":"The Six Stones and the River · 3 of 9","body":"At Chungliyimti they learned the whole of living: which seed goes in when, how a village council sits, which songs belong to which season. They grew many. And a ridge, however good, only holds so many houses and so many fields.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ao-six-stones.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ao-six-stones|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Six Stones and the River","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ao-six-stones-3":{"src":"story:fk.ao-six-stones:scenes:3:text","title":"The Six Stones and the River · 4 of 9","body":"Across the Dikhu river lay hills nobody had taken — green, folded, empty as a held breath. But the Dikhu is quick and cold, and there was no way over. So they made one: a bridge of woven cane, hung from bank to bank, the kind these hills still know how to build.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ao-six-stones.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ao-six-stones|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Six Stones and the River","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ao-six-stones-5":{"src":"story:fk.ao-six-stones:scenes:5:text","title":"The Six Stones and the River · 6 of 9","body":"They crossed — children on backs, seed baskets tied tight, the bridge swaying like a slow drum under a whole people. And those who went over took a name from the going. Ao, in their tongue, carries the sense of \"those who went across.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ao-six-stones.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ao-six-stones|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Six Stones and the River","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ao-six-stones-6":{"src":"story:fk.ao-six-stones:scenes:6:text","title":"The Six Stones and the River · 7 of 9","body":"Not everyone crossed. Some stayed on the old side of the water, and their children's children live there still, other peoples with their own names and their own tellings. The river did not divide family from stranger. It only wrote down where each family stood that morning.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ao-six-stones.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ao-six-stones|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Six Stones and the River","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.ao-six-stones-7":{"src":"story:fk.ao-six-stones:scenes:7:text","title":"The Six Stones and the River · 8 of 9","body":"On the new side, the Ao spread along the ridges and built their villages high, where the air is clean and you can see who is coming, and every village kept the story of the stones — because a people who know where they began are very hard to scatter.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-ao-six-stones.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.ao-six-stones|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Six Stones and the River","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.makhel-pear-1":{"src":"story:fk.makhel-pear:scenes:1:text","title":"The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye · 2 of 9","body":"At Makhel they were one hearth-people: one language, one way of planting, weddings within shouting distance of everybody's grandmother. And they did well — so well that the fields ran out before the children did.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-makhel-pear.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.makhel-pear|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.makhel-pear-2":{"src":"story:fk.makhel-pear:scenes:2:text","title":"The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye · 3 of 9","body":"The elders sat, the way elders sit when the news is big. The hills around were wide and empty and could feed everyone — but only if the people stopped being one village and became many. Which meant the unthinkable: parting.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-makhel-pear.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.makhel-pear|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.makhel-pear-3":{"src":"story:fk.makhel-pear:scenes:3:text","title":"The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye · 4 of 9","body":"And parting frightened them more than hunger did. Not the walking — the forgetting. Give it three generations, somebody said, and our children's children will pass each other on a path and not know they are kin.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-makhel-pear.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.makhel-pear|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.makhel-pear-5":{"src":"story:fk.makhel-pear:scenes:5:text","title":"The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye · 6 of 9","body":"Then they went — clan by clan, up different valleys, over different passes. In time their speech drifted apart, their weaves took different patterns, their villages got different names for the same moon. They became the many peoples they are today.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-makhel-pear.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.makhel-pear|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.makhel-pear-6":{"src":"story:fk.makhel-pear:scenes:6:text","title":"The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye · 7 of 9","body":"But the pear tree stood. And here is the part that should raise the hair on your arms: it stands now. And when a branch of that old tree falls, villages that trace themselves to Makhel — across all those hills, in all those languages — keep a day of rest for it, like family marking family news.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-makhel-pear.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.makhel-pear|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.makhel-pear-7":{"src":"story:fk.makhel-pear:scenes:7:text","title":"The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye · 8 of 9","body":"Think of that. A message that needs no wire and no letter: the tree at Makhel has lost a branch, and hillsides a week's walk apart go quiet on the same day, because a promise made before anyone's great-great-grandmother is still being kept.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-makhel-pear.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.makhel-pear|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Tree Where Everyone Said Goodbye","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naga-rooster-sun-1":{"src":"story:fk.naga-rooster-sun:scenes:1:text","title":"The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment · 2 of 9","body":"The hills went grey and stayed grey. The rice sulked in the fields. People got up in the dark, worked in the dark, and started forgetting which meal was which. Something had to be done, and the animals — as usual — held a meeting about it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naga-rooster-sun.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naga-rooster-sun|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naga-rooster-sun-2":{"src":"story:fk.naga-rooster-sun:scenes:2:text","title":"The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment · 3 of 9","body":"\"She has overslept,\" said the tiger. \"I will wake her.\" And he climbed the highest ridge and roared at the sky until the bamboo rattled. The sun pulled the clouds over her head like a blanket and did not so much as stir.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naga-rooster-sun.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naga-rooster-sun|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naga-rooster-sun-3":{"src":"story:fk.naga-rooster-sun:scenes:3:text","title":"The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment · 4 of 9","body":"The elephant trumpeted from the valley floor. The bear huffed. The great hornbill clapped his wings like falling trees. It was the loudest morning in the history of the hills, and it produced no morning whatsoever.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naga-rooster-sun.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naga-rooster-sun|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naga-rooster-sun-5":{"src":"story:fk.naga-rooster-sun:scenes:5:text","title":"The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment · 6 of 9","body":"The rooster climbed to the ridge, faced east, and said, at a reasonable volume: \"Grandmother Sun. Nobody has ever helped you keep the time, and that was our fault, not yours. Suppose I call you? Every morning, without fail. You would never have to guess again.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naga-rooster-sun.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naga-rooster-sun|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naga-rooster-sun-6":{"src":"story:fk.naga-rooster-sun:scenes:6:text","title":"The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment · 7 of 9","body":"There was a long pause. Then the clouds thinned, just a little, the way a blanket lifts when someone under it is listening. \"Every morning?\" said the sun. \"Without fail?\" \"Without fail,\" said the rooster. \"It will be the whole of my job, and I will do it proudly.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naga-rooster-sun.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naga-rooster-sun|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.naga-rooster-sun-7":{"src":"story:fk.naga-rooster-sun:scenes:7:text","title":"The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment · 8 of 9","body":"And that was the bargain. He calls; she comes. It is why the rooster stands so straight and wears his comb like a little red flag — he is not showing off, he is on duty. And it is why, in an Angami village, the first crow of the rooster is the real start of the day, whatever any clock says.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-naga-rooster-sun.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.naga-rooster-sun|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Rooster Who Keeps the Sun's Appointment","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-honour-1":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-honour:scenes:1:text","title":"Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned · 2 of 9","body":"A boy asked his grandfather about it, because grandfathers are for that. \"Peacocks are brighter,\" he said. \"Eagles are fiercer. Why is the honour feather a hornbill's?\" His grandfather said: \"Because of what the hornbill does that nobody watches. Come. I will show you a nest.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-honour.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-honour|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-honour-2":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-honour:scenes:2:text","title":"Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned · 3 of 9","body":"High in a great old tree there was a hole, and the hole was walled up with mud until only a slit was left. \"The mother is inside,\" said his grandfather. \"She sealed herself in, with the eggs. She will not come out for months. She cannot come out. She has given up the whole sky on purpose.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-honour.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-honour|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-honour-3":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-honour:scenes:3:text","title":"Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned · 4 of 9","body":"And as they watched, the great male came beating up the valley — a metre of black and white with a wingbeat you can hear before you see him — and landed at the slit, and passed in fruit, one piece at a time, from his own throat. Then he flew off to find more.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-honour.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-honour|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-honour-5":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-honour:scenes:5:text","title":"Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned · 6 of 9","body":"The boy looked at the slit in the tree for a long time.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-honour.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-honour|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-honour-6":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-honour:scenes:6:text","title":"Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned · 7 of 9","body":"\"So when a man wore that feather,\" said his grandfather, \"the village was saying: this one's word holds like the hornbill's. He will come back with the fruit. In these hills that was worth more than gold, because in these hills we lived by each other's word.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-honour.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-honour|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-honour-7":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-honour:scenes:7:text","title":"Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned · 8 of 9","body":"Months later the boy saw the mud wall broken open and the whole family out on the branch — the mother thin and glorious, the young ones loud, the father looking, frankly, exhausted. Nobody gave him a feather. He was the feather.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-honour.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-honour|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why the Hornbill Feather Is Earned","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-kisama-1":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-kisama:scenes:1:text","title":"The Festival of Festivals · 2 of 9","body":"For most of the year, each people keeps its own feasts in its own hills. But in December, something else happens. On a hillside called Kisama, near Kohima, a village stands that belongs to no one people — because it belongs to all of them. A morung, a great house, for each community, side by side in one row.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-kisama.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-kisama|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of Festivals","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-kisama-2":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-kisama:scenes:2:text","title":"The Festival of Festivals · 3 of 9","body":"This is the Hornbill Festival. For ten days the whole state comes to Kisama: log drums answering each other across the slope, dancers in a dozen different reds and blacks, wrestlers, singers, fire-makers, and food from every kitchen tradition in the hills, all cooking at once.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-kisama.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-kisama|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of Festivals","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-kisama-3":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-kisama:scenes:3:text","title":"The Festival of Festivals · 4 of 9","body":"A girl came with her father, and stood in the middle of it, turning slowly. \"I can hear five languages from this exact spot,\" she said. \"Six,\" said her father. \"The aunties behind you switched.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-kisama.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-kisama|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of Festivals","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-kisama-5":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-kisama:scenes:5:text","title":"The Festival of Festivals · 6 of 9","body":"The hornbill flies through the songs and stories of every Naga people — each one differently, and that is the point. The festival did not ask anyone to become alike. It asked everyone to bring what they already were, and put it in a row where the others could admire it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-kisama.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-kisama|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of Festivals","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-kisama-6":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-kisama:scenes:6:text","title":"The Festival of Festivals · 7 of 9","body":"So the girl ate Konyak food outside an Ao morung while Chakhesang drummers played, and nobody thought that was strange, because for ten days in December that is exactly what Kisama is for.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-kisama.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-kisama|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of Festivals","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.hornbill-kisama-7":{"src":"story:fk.hornbill-kisama:scenes:7:text","title":"The Festival of Festivals · 8 of 9","body":"When they left, her father bought her a shawl — from a weaver of a community not their own, who explained the pattern, and what it meant, and who was allowed to wear it at home, and why this plainer one was the right one for a guest. She kept it for years. She keeps it still.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-hornbill-kisama.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.hornbill-kisama|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival of Festivals","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.cat-tiger-lesson-1":{"src":"story:fk.cat-tiger-lesson:scenes:1:text","title":"The Lesson the Cat Kept Back · 2 of 9","body":"The tiger came to her, enormous and embarrassed. \"Teach me,\" he said. \"I have the claws and I have the shoulders and I catch nothing at all.\" And the cat, who took teaching seriously, looked him over and said: \"Very well. Lesson one: stop walking like a landslide.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cat-tiger-lesson.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cat-tiger-lesson|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lesson the Cat Kept Back","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.cat-tiger-lesson-2":{"src":"story:fk.cat-tiger-lesson:scenes:2:text","title":"The Lesson the Cat Kept Back · 3 of 9","body":"She taught him properly, the way a good teacher does. The soft walk, paw by paw. The freeze, and the patience inside the freeze. The low flow through the grass. The gathering of the back legs, and the spring. He was a good student, and she told him so, which teachers should.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cat-tiger-lesson.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cat-tiger-lesson|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lesson the Cat Kept Back","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.cat-tiger-lesson-3":{"src":"story:fk.cat-tiger-lesson:scenes:3:text","title":"The Lesson the Cat Kept Back · 4 of 9","body":"Season by season the tiger got better, and then he got magnificent. He moved like smoke. He waited like a stone. The whole forest reorganised itself around the fact of him. And somewhere in there, he began to look at his small teacher differently.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cat-tiger-lesson.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cat-tiger-lesson|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lesson the Cat Kept Back","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.cat-tiger-lesson-5":{"src":"story:fk.cat-tiger-lesson:scenes:5:text","title":"The Lesson the Cat Kept Back · 6 of 9","body":"His paws hit the empty grass. He looked left, right — then up. And there was his teacher, settled comfortably on a branch, washing an ear, entirely out of reach.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cat-tiger-lesson.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cat-tiger-lesson|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lesson the Cat Kept Back","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.cat-tiger-lesson-6":{"src":"story:fk.cat-tiger-lesson:scenes:6:text","title":"The Lesson the Cat Kept Back · 7 of 9","body":"\"You never taught me the climb,\" said the tiger, from below. \"No,\" agreed the cat. \"I kept that one. I have been keeping it for exactly this afternoon.\" And she finished her ear at leisure while the biggest of all her students sat at the bottom of the tree and thought about his manners.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cat-tiger-lesson.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cat-tiger-lesson|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lesson the Cat Kept Back","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.cat-tiger-lesson-7":{"src":"story:fk.cat-tiger-lesson:scenes:7:text","title":"The Lesson the Cat Kept Back · 8 of 9","body":"That is why, to this day, the cat climbs and the tiger cannot — and why, some elders add with a smile, a cat always looks so pleased with herself. She is the only teacher whose worst student never caught her.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-cat-tiger-lesson.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.cat-tiger-lesson|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lesson the Cat Kept Back","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sungkong-drum-1":{"src":"story:fk.sungkong-drum:scenes:1:text","title":"The Drum the Whole Village Pulled · 2 of 9","body":"Making one begins with respect. The tree is chosen carefully and asked properly — the old rites are said over it before an axe ever touches it — because you do not just take a giant. You invite one.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sungkong-drum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sungkong-drum|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Drum the Whole Village Pulled","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sungkong-drum-2":{"src":"story:fk.sungkong-drum:scenes:2:text","title":"The Drum the Whole Village Pulled · 3 of 9","body":"The carvers work where the tree fell, up in the forest: hollowing the belly, shaping the head, cutting the long slit that will be its mouth. And then comes the problem that makes this story a story. The finished drum weighs as much as a house. And it is a day's haul from home.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sungkong-drum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sungkong-drum|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Drum the Whole Village Pulled","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sungkong-drum-3":{"src":"story:fk.sungkong-drum:scenes:3:text","title":"The Drum the Whole Village Pulled · 4 of 9","body":"A boy on his first drum-hauling looked at the great thing lying in the leaves, and at the coils of cane rope, and did the arithmetic of a sensible child.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sungkong-drum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sungkong-drum|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Drum the Whole Village Pulled","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sungkong-drum-5":{"src":"story:fk.sungkong-drum:scenes:5:text","title":"The Drum the Whole Village Pulled · 6 of 9","body":"It took all day, and it was the best day of the year. Because a village that has hauled a drum together knows something about itself afterwards: there is nothing on this ridge we cannot move, if we pull on the same word.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sungkong-drum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sungkong-drum|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Drum the Whole Village Pulled","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sungkong-drum-6":{"src":"story:fk.sungkong-drum:scenes:6:text","title":"The Drum the Whole Village Pulled · 7 of 9","body":"Home, and set in its place of honour, the drum began its real work. Different beats for different news: one rhythm for danger, one for a feast, one for triumph, one for calling the village in. \"It is a voice,\" the boy's grandfather told him. \"Learn its words like anybody's words.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sungkong-drum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sungkong-drum|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Drum the Whole Village Pulled","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.sungkong-drum-7":{"src":"story:fk.sungkong-drum:scenes:7:text","title":"The Drum the Whole Village Pulled · 8 of 9","body":"And so a child of that village grew up bilingual in a language with no mouth — lying in bed, reading the valley by drumbeat: that is only practice; that is a feast at the far village; that, sit up, is news.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-sungkong-drum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.sungkong-drum|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Drum the Whole Village Pulled","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bomong-bong-1":{"src":"story:fk.bomong-bong:scenes:1:text","title":"The Two Sun Sisters · 2 of 9","body":"But two suns is one sun too many. When Bomong went down in the west, Bong came up in the east, and there was no night at all — and no rest, and no cool, and no dew. The rocks were warm at midnight. The rivers ran shallow. The world squinted all day and then squinted all night.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bomong-bong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bomong-bong|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Sun Sisters","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bomong-bong-2":{"src":"story:fk.bomong-bong:scenes:2:text","title":"The Two Sun Sisters · 3 of 9","body":"The creatures of the young world gathered in whatever shade there was and talked it over. Nobody was angry with the sisters — how can you be angry at somebody for shining? But everybody was tired, down in their bones, the way you are tired when there has never once been a night.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bomong-bong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bomong-bong|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Sun Sisters","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bomong-bong-3":{"src":"story:fk.bomong-bong:scenes:3:text","title":"The Two Sun Sisters · 4 of 9","body":"So they sent a messenger up — in this telling, a small and sensible one — to put the problem to the sisters honestly.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bomong-bong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bomong-bong|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Sun Sisters","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bomong-bong-5":{"src":"story:fk.bomong-bong:scenes:5:text","title":"The Two Sun Sisters · 6 of 9","body":"Bong let her fire cool. Down and down she turned it, from white to gold to a soft, patient silver — until she could be looked at, and then until she was lovely to look at. And she took the night watch, so her sister could keep the day.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bomong-bong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bomong-bong|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Sun Sisters","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bomong-bong-6":{"src":"story:fk.bomong-bong:scenes:6:text","title":"The Two Sun Sisters · 7 of 9","body":"That first night was the first night there had ever been. The dew came out of nowhere and sat on everything like a blessing. The creatures slept — really slept — and the rivers rose, and the world learned the trick it has kept ever since: bright, then gentle, then bright again.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bomong-bong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bomong-bong|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Sun Sisters","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.bomong-bong-7":{"src":"story:fk.bomong-bong:scenes:7:text","title":"The Two Sun Sisters · 8 of 9","body":"And the sisters still keep it. Bomong crosses by day with the fire, and Bong crosses by night with the silver, and between them nothing on earth is ever out of a sister's sight.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-bomong-bong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.bomong-bong|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Two Sun Sisters","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.donyi-polo-1":{"src":"story:fk.donyi-polo:scenes:1:text","title":"The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night · 2 of 9","body":"So one evening the boy asked. And his grandfather smiled the smile of a man who has been waiting years for a particular question. \"Donyi,\" he said. \"The sun. And Polo, the moon. Sit down. This is not a small answer.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-donyi-polo.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.donyi-polo|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.donyi-polo-2":{"src":"story:fk.donyi-polo:scenes:2:text","title":"The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night · 3 of 9","body":"\"We are Tani people — Galo, and the Adi, the Nyishi, the Apatani, the Tagin, all the children of Abo Tani. And this is how we hold the world: Donyi and Polo, the sun and the moon, are the two eyes of the great truth. The seeing of it. By day, nothing you do is unseen. By night, nothing either.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-donyi-polo.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.donyi-polo|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.donyi-polo-3":{"src":"story:fk.donyi-polo:scenes:3:text","title":"The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night · 4 of 9","body":"\"Not spying,\" he added, because the boy's face had asked. \"A mother watching the courtyard is not spying. Donyi sees the way a mother sees — everything, and kindly, and with very little patience for lying. That is why we point to her when we promise. We are saying: she heard me too.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-donyi-polo.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.donyi-polo|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.donyi-polo-5":{"src":"story:fk.donyi-polo:scenes:5:text","title":"The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night · 6 of 9","body":"\"And because she sees truly,\" his grandfather went on, \"we try to live so that being seen is no trouble to us. That is the whole of it, really. Speak straight. Deal straight. Walk as if it is always noon.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-donyi-polo.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.donyi-polo|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.donyi-polo-6":{"src":"story:fk.donyi-polo:scenes:6:text","title":"The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night · 7 of 9","body":"The boy learned the rest the way children learn: at festivals, at prayers, in the gangging — the prayer halls where Donyi-Polo families now gather to sing. He learned that his people had held this faith since before anyone counted, and that it had a flag with a sun on it, and songs he already half knew.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-donyi-polo.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.donyi-polo|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.donyi-polo-7":{"src":"story:fk.donyi-polo:scenes:7:text","title":"The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night · 8 of 9","body":"And he began doing the thing himself — the small lift of the chin, sunward, when he gave his word to a friend. It changed the promises. Knowing you are seen, and loved anyway, and expected to be honest: it turns out that is a very good way to grow up.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-donyi-polo.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.donyi-polo|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Eye of the Day, the Eye of the Night","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kine-nane-1":{"src":"story:fk.kine-nane:scenes:1:text","title":"The Mother of the Grain · 2 of 8","body":"The grain existed. But it was not with people. In the Adi tellings the grain belonged with Kine Nane, the mother of crops, the one in whose keeping every seed of it lay — and the distance between her storehouse and a hungry village was the whole problem of the world.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kine-nane.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kine-nane|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother of the Grain","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kine-nane-2":{"src":"story:fk.kine-nane:scenes:2:text","title":"The Mother of the Grain · 3 of 8","body":"The tellings of how the rice crossed that distance differ from valley to valley — in some it is begged for, in some it is carried by a creature, in some the getting of it costs Kine Nane dearly, and the elders do not hurry past that part. What every telling keeps is this: the rice came to people as a gift with a weight to it. Not a finding. A giving.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kine-nane.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kine-nane|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother of the Grain","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kine-nane-3":{"src":"story:fk.kine-nane:scenes:3:text","title":"The Mother of the Grain · 4 of 8","body":"And so, one spring, there was seed in a human hand for the first time. A small heap of it. Enough, maybe, to fill every belly in the village for one single glorious evening.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kine-nane.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kine-nane|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother of the Grain","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kine-nane-5":{"src":"story:fk.kine-nane:scenes:5:text","title":"The Mother of the Grain · 6 of 8","body":"And they did not forget where it had come from. The first of every harvest went back — set out for Kine Nane before anyone ate, with thanks said properly. \"The field is ours to work,\" the elders said. \"The grain is hers to give. Keep the order of that straight and the order of everything stays straight.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kine-nane.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kine-nane|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother of the Grain","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kine-nane-6":{"src":"story:fk.kine-nane:scenes:6:text","title":"The Mother of the Grain · 7 of 8","body":"That thanks grew into a festival. At Solung, in the Adi villages, the harvest's gratitude is kept — the rites said, the food shared out, Kine Nane remembered by name — a whole festival that is, at its heart, a thank-you letter that has been sent every year for centuries.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kine-nane.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kine-nane|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Mother of the Grain","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.nyokum-1":{"src":"story:fk.nyokum:scenes:1:text","title":"The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited · 2 of 8","body":"A girl watched her first Nyokum being prepared: the ground swept, the altar of bamboo raised — a beautiful, intricate thing, fresh-cut and pale gold — and her grandmother explaining the guest list, which was unlike any guest list she had ever heard.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-nyokum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.nyokum|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.nyokum-2":{"src":"story:fk.nyokum:scenes:2:text","title":"The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited · 3 of 8","body":"\"Everyone is invited,\" said her grandmother. \"And I mean everyone. Every family, of course — but also the spirits of the rivers. The spirits of the hills. The forest ones. The house ones. The ones we have names for and the ones we do not. Nyokum is not a festival with a door.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-nyokum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.nyokum|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.nyokum-3":{"src":"story:fk.nyokum:scenes:3:text","title":"The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited · 4 of 8","body":"The girl thought about parties she knew of, which mostly worked the other way — lists of who was in, which meant lists of who was out.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-nyokum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.nyokum|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.nyokum-5":{"src":"story:fk.nyokum:scenes:5:text","title":"The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited · 6 of 8","body":"And then it was a festival, with everything that word should mean: dancing in a great circle that anyone could join and everyone did, songs traded between villages, food that had been days in the making, and the particular happiness of a place where nobody present was unwelcome.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-nyokum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.nyokum|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.nyokum-6":{"src":"story:fk.nyokum:scenes:6:text","title":"The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited · 7 of 8","body":"\"This is how we hold the world,\" her grandmother said, resting at the edge of the dance. \"People are not the owners of the land, and not its masters. We are one set of neighbours in it. Nyokum is the day the whole neighbourhood — seen and unseen — sits down to eat together.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-nyokum.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.nyokum|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Festival Where Everyone Is Invited","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-taki-1":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-taki:scenes:1:text","title":"Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed · 2 of 9","body":"A spirit came to Abo Tani at planting time, friendly as anything, with a basket on his arm. \"Neighbour! Your seed looks small and dusty this year. Mine is fat and shining. Trade with me, basket for basket — I like you, and I am feeling generous.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-taki.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-taki|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-taki-2":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-taki:scenes:2:text","title":"Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed · 3 of 9","body":"And the spirit's seed WAS beautiful — plump, glossy, smelling faintly and pleasantly of toasted grain. Abo Tani, who had been flattered before breakfast and never quite got over it, traded on the spot. Basket for basket. The spirit went off whistling, which — again — should have been a clue.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-taki.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-taki|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-taki-3":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-taki:scenes:3:text","title":"Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed · 4 of 9","body":"Abo Tani cut his field, burned it clean, and planted every shining seed with care. The spirit, over the ridge, planted the small dusty ones. And then the rains came, and the season turned, and Abo Tani went out to see his field.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-taki.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-taki|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-taki-5":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-taki:scenes:5:text","title":"Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed · 6 of 9","body":"Abo Tani sat down at the edge of his empty field. He was angry for a while, and then — because this is what makes him Abo Tani — he got interested. How do you tell living seed from dead, before you bet a season on it?","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-taki.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-taki|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-taki-6":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-taki:scenes:6:text","title":"Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed · 7 of 9","body":"Next planting time, the spirit came back with another gleaming basket. Abo Tani thanked him warmly, took a pinch, folded it into wet moss by the fire, and invited the spirit to stay for dinner while they waited. The spirit developed an urgent appointment elsewhere.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-taki.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-taki|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-taki-7":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-taki:scenes:7:text","title":"Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed · 8 of 9","body":"And the pinch, of course, never sprouted, and the trade never happened, and Abo Tani planted his own small dusty faithful seed and ate well that year. He told the story on himself forever after, loudly, at festivals — because a lesson you paid a whole season for should at least be given away free.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-taki.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-taki|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Abo Tani and the Roasted Seed","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-sky-bride-1":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-sky-bride:scenes:1:text","title":"How Abo Tani Married the Sky · 2 of 10","body":"\"Three tasks,\" said the house of the sky. \"Clear the great hillside — in one day. Sort this basket, where three kinds of seed have been mixed together — by morning. And bring water up from the river — in a carrying-basket of open weave. Do those, and we will talk.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-sky-bride.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-sky-bride|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How Abo Tani Married the Sky","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-sky-bride-2":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-sky-bride:scenes:2:text","title":"How Abo Tani Married the Sky · 3 of 10","body":"Now, Abo Tani was clever, but he was not foolish about his own cleverness. He knew a hillside is bigger than a man, a night is shorter than a mixed basket, and water has firm opinions about baskets. So he did the thing that the proud never think of: he went and asked for help.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-sky-bride.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-sky-bride|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How Abo Tani Married the Sky","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-sky-bride-3":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-sky-bride:scenes:3:text","title":"How Abo Tani Married the Sky · 4 of 10","body":"And here the tellings smile, because Abo Tani had spent his whole life being decent to small things — sharing his rice with the ants, leaving the wild bees their fair half, mending a wing here and a burrow there — never once thinking of it as an investment.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-sky-bride.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-sky-bride|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How Abo Tani Married the Sky","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-sky-bride-4":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-sky-bride:scenes:4:text","title":"How Abo Tani Married the Sky · 5 of 10","body":"The wind, who owed him a kindness, lay down on the great hillside and rolled, and the clearing of one day was done in an afternoon. And in the night, the ants came to the mixed basket in their thousands — and ants, as anyone who has dropped a sweet knows, can sort anything from anything.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-sky-bride.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-sky-bride|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How Abo Tani Married the Sky","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-sky-bride-6":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-sky-bride:scenes:6:text","title":"How Abo Tani Married the Sky · 7 of 10","body":"The house of the sky looked at the cleared hill, the sorted basket, the impossible water. \"You did not do these alone,\" said the sky. \"No,\" said Abo Tani cheerfully. \"Nobody does anything alone. I thought that was the test.\" And the sky's house, the tellings say, laughed — because it was.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-sky-bride.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-sky-bride|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How Abo Tani Married the Sky","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-sky-bride-7":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-sky-bride:scenes:7:text","title":"How Abo Tani Married the Sky · 8 of 10","body":"So Abo Tani married his sky bride, with the wind and the ants at the wedding and the moss, presumably, somewhere near the water pots. And their children, and their children's children, became the Tani peoples — the Adi, the Nyishi, the Apatani, the Galo, the Tagin.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-sky-bride.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-sky-bride|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How Abo Tani Married the Sky","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.abotani-sky-bride-8":{"src":"story:fk.abotani-sky-bride:scenes:8:text","title":"How Abo Tani Married the Sky · 9 of 10","body":"Which is a thing worth saying slowly: in these tellings, every Tani person alive is descended from the earth on one side and the sky's own house on the other. Half hill, half light. It explains a lot, their elders say.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-abotani-sky-bride.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.abotani-sky-bride|s8","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from How Abo Tani Married the Sky","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-fk.tawang-horse-1":{"src":"story:fk.tawang-horse:scenes:1:text","title":"The Horse Who Chose the Monastery · 2 of 9","body":"More than three hundred years ago, a lama called Mera Lama was charged with building a great monastery in these mountains. A monastery is not put just anywhere. It must have water, and shelter from the worst winds, and ground that can hold it, and something harder to name — a rightness.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tawang-horse.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tawang-horse|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Horse Who Chose the Monastery","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tawang-horse-2":{"src":"story:fk.tawang-horse:scenes:2:text","title":"The Horse Who Chose the Monastery · 3 of 9","body":"Mera Lama searched. Ridge after ridge, valley after valley, he weighed each place and each place was almost right, which is the most tiring kind of wrong. And one evening, deep in the search, he stopped to pray — and when he rose from his prayers, his horse was gone.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tawang-horse.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tawang-horse|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Horse Who Chose the Monastery","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tawang-horse-3":{"src":"story:fk.tawang-horse:scenes:3:text","title":"The Horse Who Chose the Monastery · 4 of 9","body":"You may lose many things lightly in the high mountains. A horse is not one of them. He went after it on foot — up through the rhododendrons, over a shoulder of hill, following the prints, and the light going all the while.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tawang-horse.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tawang-horse|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Horse Who Chose the Monastery","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tawang-horse-5":{"src":"story:fk.tawang-horse:scenes:5:text","title":"The Horse Who Chose the Monastery · 6 of 9","body":"Water near. The wind broken by the ridge at its back. The valleys spread below like an open book, and the high peaks standing witness behind. Every almost-right place he had weighed all those months — and his horse had walked off, without a single opinion in its head, and stood down in the middle of the rightest place in the mountains.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tawang-horse.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tawang-horse|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Horse Who Chose the Monastery","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tawang-horse-6":{"src":"story:fk.tawang-horse:scenes:6:text","title":"The Horse Who Chose the Monastery · 7 of 9","body":"There the monastery was built, and there it stands — and its name remembers the whole story. Tawang: chosen by horse. Ta for the horse, wang for the choosing. Ask a Monpa what the name of their great monastery means and they will tell you about a lama who was wise enough to take a horse's advice.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tawang-horse.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tawang-horse|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Horse Who Chose the Monastery","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tawang-horse-7":{"src":"story:fk.tawang-horse:scenes:7:text","title":"The Horse Who Chose the Monastery · 8 of 9","body":"Today hundreds of monks live and study at Tawang, and the butter lamps burn, and the great prayer hall holds a Buddha figure taller than a house. And somewhere in the story of all of it is an evening, a prayer, and a horse quietly walking away.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tawang-horse.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tawang-horse|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Horse Who Chose the Monastery","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.teesta-rangeet-1":{"src":"story:fk.teesta-rangeet:scenes:1:text","title":"The Race of the Two Rivers · 2 of 9","body":"But a river setting out for the first time does not know the way down — nobody had ever gone down before. So each took a guide. Rongnyu chose Paril bu, the snake. Rongeet chose Tut fo, a mountain bird. And you can already feel the story leaning, can't you.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-teesta-rangeet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.teesta-rangeet|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race of the Two Rivers","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.teesta-rangeet-2":{"src":"story:fk.teesta-rangeet:scenes:2:text","title":"The Race of the Two Rivers · 3 of 9","body":"The snake went as snakes go: smooth, patient, low, choosing the fall of the land and keeping to it. And Rongnyu followed him down in long easy curves, gathering streams as she went, arriving in the valley calm and full and early.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-teesta-rangeet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.teesta-rangeet|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race of the Two Rivers","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.teesta-rangeet-3":{"src":"story:fk.teesta-rangeet:scenes:3:text","title":"The Race of the Two Rivers · 4 of 9","body":"The bird went as birds go. He saw a ridge worth looking at and looked at it. He fancied a berry and detoured for it. He forgot, for whole afternoons, that anyone was following him at all. And behind him Rongeet went crashing and doubling through the gorges, taking every wrong valley the bird's fancy took.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-teesta-rangeet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.teesta-rangeet|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race of the Two Rivers","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.teesta-rangeet-5":{"src":"story:fk.teesta-rangeet:scenes:5:text","title":"The Race of the Two Rivers · 6 of 9","body":"And in his hurt pride, Rongeet reared back and began to turn — to flow backwards, uphill, away — and the waters rose and trembled through the whole valley.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-teesta-rangeet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.teesta-rangeet|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race of the Two Rivers","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.teesta-rangeet-6":{"src":"story:fk.teesta-rangeet:scenes:6:text","title":"The Race of the Two Rivers · 7 of 9","body":"The waters settled. Rongeet came the last mile gently, and the two rivers met — and they have never parted since. You can stand today at the meeting place below Melli and watch them join: two colours of water, sliding into one.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-teesta-rangeet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.teesta-rangeet|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race of the Two Rivers","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.teesta-rangeet-7":{"src":"story:fk.teesta-rangeet:scenes:7:text","title":"The Race of the Two Rivers · 8 of 9","body":"And the Lepcha say the valley itself was glad. At the joining of the rivers, to this day, the air fills with butterflies — clouds of them, drifting over the water where Rongnyu and Rongeet met. Go and see. They are there.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-teesta-rangeet.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.teesta-rangeet|s7","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Race of the Two Rivers","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tendong-hill-1":{"src":"story:fk.tendong-hill:scenes:1:text","title":"The Hill That Held Its People Up · 2 of 8","body":"The people climbed, because that is all you can do. Hill after hill went under behind them. And the tellings are honest about how that felt: the world they knew was being folded away under grey water, and nobody could say where the folding would stop.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tendong-hill.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tendong-hill|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Held Its People Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tendong-hill-2":{"src":"story:fk.tendong-hill:scenes:2:text","title":"The Hill That Held Its People Up · 3 of 8","body":"Then, above the drowned valleys, one hill did what no hill does. Tendong, in the south of Sikkim, rose. As the water climbed, the hill climbed — lifting itself, and everyone on it, above the flood, patient as a grandfather raising a child over a puddle.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tendong-hill.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tendong-hill|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Held Its People Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tendong-hill-3":{"src":"story:fk.tendong-hill:scenes:3:text","title":"The Hill That Held Its People Up · 4 of 8","body":"For days the hilltop was the whole country — a village of everyone, perched between grey water and grey sky, keeping each other warm and keeping the little ones from the edge, while below them Tendong held, and held, and held.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tendong-hill.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tendong-hill|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Held Its People Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tendong-hill-5":{"src":"story:fk.tendong-hill:scenes:5:text","title":"The Hill That Held Its People Up · 6 of 8","body":"They did not forget. You do not forget the ground that held you up. Every year since — every single year, down all the generations — the Lepcha have climbed to thank the hill. The festival is called Tendong Lho Rum Faat: the worship of the Tendong hill. It is kept still, each August.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tendong-hill.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tendong-hill|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Held Its People Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.tendong-hill-6":{"src":"story:fk.tendong-hill:scenes:6:text","title":"The Hill That Held Its People Up · 7 of 8","body":"Families go up together, the old rites are said, and the children are told this story on the hill it happened on — which is the best classroom any story ever had.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-tendong-hill.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.tendong-hill|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Hill That Held Its People Up","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.khangchendzonga-five-1":{"src":"story:fk.khangchendzonga-five:scenes:1:text","title":"The Five Treasures of the Great Snow · 2 of 8","body":"Five summits, five treasuries. The tellings usually count them so: gold, and silver, and precious stones; grain; and sacred books — the holy knowledge. Some add salt, some add medicines. Locked in the mountain, all of it, under the deepest snow on earth.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khangchendzonga-five.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khangchendzonga-five|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Five Treasures of the Great Snow","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.khangchendzonga-five-2":{"src":"story:fk.khangchendzonga-five:scenes:2:text","title":"The Five Treasures of the Great Snow · 3 of 8","body":"And the treasuries have a keeper. Dzönga, the guardian god of Sikkim, rides the high snows and watches over the land — you can see him danced at the festival of Pang Lhabsol, masked in red, magnificent, the mountain itself given a face for a day.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khangchendzonga-five.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khangchendzonga-five|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Five Treasures of the Great Snow","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.khangchendzonga-five-3":{"src":"story:fk.khangchendzonga-five:scenes:3:text","title":"The Five Treasures of the Great Snow · 4 of 8","body":"Now here is the question a child in Sikkim eventually asks, and it is the right question: if the mountain is full of gold and grain, why does nobody go and get it?","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khangchendzonga-five.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khangchendzonga-five|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Five Treasures of the Great Snow","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.khangchendzonga-five-5":{"src":"story:fk.khangchendzonga-five:scenes:5:text","title":"The Five Treasures of the Great Snow · 6 of 8","body":"It changes how you look at the mountain. On a clear morning the whole of Sikkim can see it — and what they are seeing, the old tradition says, is the world's emergency store, kept faithfully by its guardian, needed by nobody yet. Every sunrise on those five summits is the good news that the worst day has still not come.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khangchendzonga-five.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khangchendzonga-five|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Five Treasures of the Great Snow","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.khangchendzonga-five-6":{"src":"story:fk.khangchendzonga-five:scenes:6:text","title":"The Five Treasures of the Great Snow · 7 of 8","body":"It is why the summit is not stood upon. When climbers first reached the top in 1955, they stopped short of the very summit out of respect, and most have done the same since. You may visit the treasury's doorstep. You do not stand on the roof of the vault.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-khangchendzonga-five.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.khangchendzonga-five|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Five Treasures of the Great Snow","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kabi-lungchok-1":{"src":"story:fk.kabi-lungchok:scenes:1:text","title":"The Brothers of the Standing Stones · 2 of 8","body":"And everyone knows the usual next chapter of that story, because the world has told it a thousand times: suspicion, then quarrels, then worse. The leaders of the two peoples — Thekong Tek, the great Lepcha elder, and Khye Bumsa, the Bhutia chief — could each see that chapter coming.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kabi-lungchok.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kabi-lungchok|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Brothers of the Standing Stones","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kabi-lungchok-2":{"src":"story:fk.kabi-lungchok:scenes:2:text","title":"The Brothers of the Standing Stones · 3 of 8","body":"So they chose to write a different one. They met at a place called Kabi Lungchok, in the north, in a clearing among the trees — not to trade, not to divide the valleys up, but to do something bigger: to become brothers. Not friends. Brothers. Formally, permanently, both peoples at once.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kabi-lungchok.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kabi-lungchok|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Brothers of the Standing Stones","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kabi-lungchok-3":{"src":"story:fk.kabi-lungchok:scenes:3:text","title":"The Brothers of the Standing Stones · 4 of 8","body":"With the mountain standing witness over the trees, the two chiefs swore the bond of brothers between Lepcha and Bhutia — and they raised standing stones at Kabi Lungchok to hold the oath in place, the way you would set a weight on an important paper, except the weight was granite and the paper was forever.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kabi-lungchok.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kabi-lungchok|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Brothers of the Standing Stones","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kabi-lungchok-5":{"src":"story:fk.kabi-lungchok:scenes:5:text","title":"The Brothers of the Standing Stones · 6 of 8","body":"Every year at Pang Lhabsol, when Sikkim honours its guardian mountain, the oath is remembered too — the festival carries both at once, the guardian and the brotherhood, because in Sikkim the two are the same memory: the day the mountain was asked to hold two peoples to their word.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kabi-lungchok.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kabi-lungchok|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Brothers of the Standing Stones","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.kabi-lungchok-6":{"src":"story:fk.kabi-lungchok:scenes:6:text","title":"The Brothers of the Standing Stones · 7 of 8","body":"And the stones are still there. You can visit Kabi Lungchok today, on the road north from Gangtok, and stand in front of them: unwritten, unreadable, and perfectly clear. Two peoples decided to be family, and told the biggest thing they knew to hold them to it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-kabi-lungchok.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.kabi-lungchok|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Brothers of the Standing Stones","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.mutanchi-1":{"src":"story:fk.mutanchi:scenes:1:text","title":"The Children of the Snowy Peak · 2 of 8","body":"In the beginning, Itbu-mu — the Mother Creator — wished for children. And she did not shape them from clay, or from wood, or from anything of the low warm valleys. She reached to the crown of Khangchendzonga and took the purest, freshest snow of its highest peaks, and from it she shaped the first man and the first woman.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mutanchi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mutanchi|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children of the Snowy Peak","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.mutanchi-2":{"src":"story:fk.mutanchi:scenes:2:text","title":"The Children of the Snowy Peak · 3 of 8","body":"Fudongthing and Nazong-nyu, the tellings name them. And from those two came the Lepcha people — which is why, when you ask a Lepcha who they are, you may hear the old name they keep for themselves: Mutanchi Rongkup. The children of the snowy peak.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mutanchi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mutanchi|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children of the Snowy Peak","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.mutanchi-3":{"src":"story:fk.mutanchi:scenes:3:text","title":"The Children of the Snowy Peak · 4 of 8","body":"Stop on that for a moment, because it changes everything it touches. If your first ancestors were shaped from the mountain's own snow, then the mountain is not scenery, and not property, and not a challenge to be climbed.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mutanchi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mutanchi|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children of the Snowy Peak","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.mutanchi-5":{"src":"story:fk.mutanchi:scenes:5:text","title":"The Children of the Snowy Peak · 6 of 8","body":"A Lepcha grandmother, telling this to her grandchildren, will put it simply: \"The mountain was here before us, and we are made of what it is made of. So the rivers are your relatives, and the forests are your relatives, and you do not steal from relatives, and you do not forget them.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mutanchi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mutanchi|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children of the Snowy Peak","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.mutanchi-6":{"src":"story:fk.mutanchi:scenes:6:text","title":"The Children of the Snowy Peak · 7 of 8","body":"When the first climbers came to stand on Khangchendzonga, Sikkim asked them to stop short of the very top — and they did, and most have since. The world calls that respecting local belief. The Lepcha would say it more warmly: guests understood they were in somebody's grandparent's house.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-mutanchi.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.mutanchi|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Children of the Snowy Peak","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.snow-keeper-1":{"src":"story:fk.snow-keeper:scenes:1:text","title":"The Shy Keeper of the High Snows · 2 of 8","body":"He goes by different names in different valleys — the wild man of the snows, the glacier one; the world outside calls him the yeti. The Lepcha, who name everything in their hills, count the spirit of the glaciers among the beings a sensible person greets from a distance. What everyone agrees on is the important part: he is shy.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-snow-keeper.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.snow-keeper|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Shy Keeper of the High Snows","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.snow-keeper-2":{"src":"story:fk.snow-keeper:scenes:2:text","title":"The Shy Keeper of the High Snows · 3 of 8","body":"Not fierce. Shy. The stories are all sideways stories: a line of great prints crossing a snowfield at dawn, going somewhere with a purpose. A whistle off a ridge in the mist, answered by nothing. A herd of blue sheep flowing round a rock as though someone stood there. Never the keeper himself. Always the space where he just was.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-snow-keeper.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.snow-keeper|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Shy Keeper of the High Snows","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.snow-keeper-3":{"src":"story:fk.snow-keeper:scenes:3:text","title":"The Shy Keeper of the High Snows · 4 of 8","body":"A boy at a high summer pasture asked his grandfather about it, the way you do when the fire is low. \"Is he real?\" His grandfather turned his tea bowl a while. \"The high snows are quiet and clean and nobody troubles them,\" he said. \"Somebody is doing that job. That is as real as most things.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-snow-keeper.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.snow-keeper|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Shy Keeper of the High Snows","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.snow-keeper-5":{"src":"story:fk.snow-keeper:scenes:5:text","title":"The Shy Keeper of the High Snows · 6 of 8","body":"\"When we walk up there,\" his grandfather said, \"we walk gently. We take our rubbish home. We do not shout on the ridges. Not because we are afraid — because it is his, the way our pasture is ours, and he has never once been a bad neighbour to us.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-snow-keeper.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.snow-keeper|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Shy Keeper of the High Snows","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.snow-keeper-6":{"src":"story:fk.snow-keeper:scenes:6:text","title":"The Shy Keeper of the High Snows · 7 of 8","body":"And that is the loveliest thing about the keeper of the snows: a being whose whole story is a lesson in leaving things be. The mountains hold him the way a pocket holds something precious — unseen, and better for it.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-snow-keeper.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.snow-keeper|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Shy Keeper of the High Snows","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.losoong-1":{"src":"story:fk.losoong:scenes:1:text","title":"The Year Is Swept Out Dancing · 2 of 8","body":"A girl spent it at her grandmother's village, and found that seeing out a year is a whole craft. The house swept end to end — properly, into the corners, \"because whatever dust you keep, you keep.\" Everything mended that could be mended. Everything returned that had been borrowed.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-losoong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.losoong|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Year Is Swept Out Dancing","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.losoong-2":{"src":"story:fk.losoong:scenes:2:text","title":"The Year Is Swept Out Dancing · 3 of 8","body":"At the monastery, in the days before, the masked cham dances turn in the courtyard — slow, magnificent figures to a heartbeat of drums and long horns. The dances face down the troubles of the old year, the villagers explained, so that none of them slink along into the new one.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-losoong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.losoong|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Year Is Swept Out Dancing","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.losoong-3":{"src":"story:fk.losoong:scenes:3:text","title":"The Year Is Swept Out Dancing · 4 of 8","body":"And then the part every child waits for: archery. Losoong without archery is not Losoong. The men of the villages compete all day — real bows, tremendous concentration, spectacular boasting — and the arrows thud home to roars you can hear across the valley.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-losoong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.losoong|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Year Is Swept Out Dancing","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.losoong-5":{"src":"story:fk.losoong:scenes:5:text","title":"The Year Is Swept Out Dancing · 6 of 8","body":"\"People carry old years around with them,\" her grandmother said, folding the last of the mending. \"Old quarrels, old sulks, old dust. Once a year, we put it all down on purpose. It takes a whole festival, because putting things down is harder than picking things up.\"","art":"art/story/sm/fk-losoong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.losoong|s5","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Year Is Swept Out Dancing","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-fk.losoong-6":{"src":"story:fk.losoong:scenes:6:text","title":"The Year Is Swept Out Dancing · 7 of 8","body":"On new year's morning the village visited itself house to house, and everyone greeted everyone, including two old neighbours the girl happened to know had quarrelled in the spring — greeting each other on a clean doorstep, in a new year, with last year swept out behind them both.","art":"art/story/sm/fk-losoong.jpg","route":"#/story/fk.losoong|s6","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Year Is Swept Out Dancing","facts":[["From","Hills of the Dawn"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.dhyanchand-1":{"src":"story:kh.dhyanchand:scenes:1:text","title":"The Wizard With the Stick · 2 of 6","body":"When India went to the Olympic Games — Amsterdam in 1928, Los Angeles in 1932, Berlin in 1936 — the world found out what all that moonlight had built. India won the gold medal every single time. Three Olympics, three golds. In one match in 1932, India beat the home team of the United States by twenty-four goals to one. That scoreline sat in the record books for generations.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-dhyanchand.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.dhyanchand|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wizard With the Stick","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.dhyanchand-2":{"src":"story:kh.dhyanchand:scenes:2:text","title":"The Wizard With the Stick · 3 of 6","body":"At the centre of it all was Dhyan Chand, gliding through defenders as if they were standing still, the ball stitched to his stick. He scored goals the way other people say hello — by the hundreds, across his career.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-dhyanchand.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.dhyanchand|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wizard With the Stick","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.dhyanchand-4":{"src":"story:kh.dhyanchand:scenes:4:text","title":"The Wizard With the Stick · 5 of 6","body":"In Berlin in 1936, in the final against Germany with the stands packed against them, India won eight goals to one. That one is no legend — it is in the books. It was the third gold, and it made Dhyan Chand something rare: an athlete other countries told stories about.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-dhyanchand.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.dhyanchand|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wizard With the Stick","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.kapil83-1":{"src":"story:kh.kapil83:scenes:1:text","title":"The Cup Nobody Expected · 2 of 6","body":"India’s captain was Kapil Dev, twenty-four years old, from a family that had settled in the north after hard journeys of its own — a fast bowler who hit the ball like a thunderclap and refused, completely, to believe the odds. The team called him the Haryana Hurricane.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-kapil83.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.kapil83|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Cup Nobody Expected","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.kapil83-2":{"src":"story:kh.kapil83:scenes:2:text","title":"The Cup Nobody Expected · 3 of 6","body":"Against Zimbabwe, India collapsed to seventeen runs for five wickets — a disaster. Kapil walked in and played one of the greatest innings ever: 175 not out, sixteen fours, six sixes. And here is the strange, wonderful footnote the record keeps: the television cameras were on strike that day. Not one ball of it was filmed.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-kapil83.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.kapil83|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Cup Nobody Expected","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.kapil83-4":{"src":"story:kh.kapil83:scenes:4:text","title":"The Cup Nobody Expected · 5 of 6","body":"After that catch, the invincible West Indies crumbled — all out for 140. India had won the World Cup by forty-three runs, and Kapil lifted the golden trophy on the Lord’s balcony while a crowd of disbelieving, delighted Indians roared below.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-kapil83.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.kapil83|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Cup Nobody Expected","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.sachin-1":{"src":"story:kh.sachin:scenes:1:text","title":"The Boy With the Ball in a Sock · 2 of 6","body":"His coach, Ramakant Achrekar, had a famous trick. At the end of practice he would balance a one-rupee coin on the stumps. Any bowler who got Sachin out won the coin. If nobody could, Sachin kept it. He kept thirteen of those coins all his life, and said they were among his most precious prizes.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-sachin.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.sachin|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy With the Ball in a Sock","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.sachin-2":{"src":"story:kh.sachin:scenes:2:text","title":"The Boy With the Ball in a Sock · 3 of 6","body":"At sixteen — sixteen! — he played for India against the fierce fast bowlers of Pakistan, took a blow, bled, and refused to leave the field. India watched a boy in a man’s game refuse to be a boy about it, and something began that day that did not stop for twenty-four years.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-sachin.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.sachin|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy With the Ball in a Sock","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.sachin-4":{"src":"story:kh.sachin:scenes:4:text","title":"The Boy With the Ball in a Sock · 5 of 6","body":"The one prize that kept escaping was the World Cup — until 2011, his sixth try, when India finally won it at home, in his own Bombay. His young teammates lifted him onto their shoulders and carried him around the ground. One of them, Virat Kohli, said that night that Sachin had carried the burden of the nation for twenty-one years, so it was time to carry him.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-sachin.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.sachin|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy With the Ball in a Sock","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.anand-1":{"src":"story:kh.anand:scenes:1:text","title":"The Lightning Kid · 2 of 6","body":"At the chess club they gave him a nickname: the Lightning Kid. He played at a speed that unsettled grown masters — glance, move, done — not out of carelessness but because he simply saw it faster. In 1987 he became world junior champion, and in 1988, at eighteen, he became the first grandmaster in the history of India.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-anand.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.anand|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lightning Kid","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.anand-2":{"src":"story:kh.anand:scenes:2:text","title":"The Lightning Kid · 3 of 6","body":"Being first is a strange job. There was no Indian who had walked this road ahead of him, no map, and the strongest players and schools were continents away. Every tournament meant travelling alone to the other side of the world to play people raised in the game’s great capitals. He kept going anyway, year after year, match after match.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-anand.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.anand|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lightning Kid","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.anand-4":{"src":"story:kh.anand:scenes:4:text","title":"The Lightning Kid · 5 of 6","body":"But the real ending of this story is not a trophy. When Anand became India’s first grandmaster, he was the only one. Today India has more than eighty, many of them from Chennai, many of them children who started because a calm, fast, smiling man from their own city had shown it could be done.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-anand.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.anand|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Lightning Kid","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.marykom-1":{"src":"story:kh.marykom:scenes:1:text","title":"Six Crowns from Kangathei · 2 of 6","body":"Privately, because girls from her village were not supposed to box, and her father feared it — the injuries, the neighbours, all of it. So she trained without telling him, walking to the gym, learning to move, learning to hit. Then she won a state championship, and her photograph appeared in the newspaper, and that is how her father found out his daughter was a boxer.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-marykom.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.marykom|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Six Crowns from Kangathei","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.marykom-2":{"src":"story:kh.marykom:scenes:2:text","title":"Six Crowns from Kangathei · 3 of 6","body":"Then the winning started and would not stop. World championship gold in 2002. Again in 2005. Again in 2006. Boxers from every continent, and again and again the small, fast woman from Kangathei with the thunder in her hands.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-marykom.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.marykom|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Six Crowns from Kangathei","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.marykom-4":{"src":"story:kh.marykom:scenes:4:text","title":"Six Crowns from Kangathei · 5 of 6","body":"In 2012, when women’s boxing entered the Olympics at last, she was there in London, the only Indian woman in the draw, and fought her way to a bronze medal for India. A girl who had trained in secret now had a whole country making noise for her.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-marykom.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.marykom|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Six Crowns from Kangathei","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.sindhu-1":{"src":"story:kh.sindhu:scenes:1:text","title":"The Girl Who Woke Before the Birds · 2 of 6","body":"Gopichand went on to open a training academy — on the far side of the city. From the age of eight, Sindhu was up before dawn, travelling tens of kilometres there and back, morning practice before school, evening practice after. Do the sums sometime: years of mornings, every one of them chosen while the rest of the city slept.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-sindhu.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.sindhu|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Woke Before the Birds","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.sindhu-2":{"src":"story:kh.sindhu:scenes:2:text","title":"The Girl Who Woke Before the Birds · 3 of 6","body":"At the Rio Olympics in 2016, at twenty-one, she fought her way to the final — the first Indian woman ever to win an Olympic silver. The whole country stayed up to watch. But silver is a strange medal: it glitters, and it whispers about the one match you did not win.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-sindhu.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.sindhu|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Woke Before the Birds","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.sindhu-4":{"src":"story:kh.sindhu:scenes:4:text","title":"The Girl Who Woke Before the Birds · 5 of 6","body":"Watch her play and you see it: the reach, the leap, the smash that sounds like a door slamming. But everyone who knows her story sees something else behind it — a small girl in a car in the Hyderabad dark, halfway to practice while the birds are still asleep.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-sindhu.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.sindhu|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Woke Before the Birds","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.neeraj-1":{"src":"story:kh.neeraj:scenes:1:text","title":"The Spear From Khandra · 2 of 6","body":"At the stadium he saw the javelin throwers — and that was that. There is a moment when a spear leaves a thrower’s hand and hangs in the air as if it may never come down, and Neeraj wanted that moment for himself. He turned out to be astonishing at it. At the world junior championships in 2016 he threw 86.48 metres — a world record for his age group. The boy from Khandra held a world record.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-neeraj.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.neeraj|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Spear From Khandra","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.neeraj-2":{"src":"story:kh.neeraj:scenes:2:text","title":"The Spear From Khandra · 3 of 6","body":"Here is the thing about the javelin: India had never won an Olympic gold medal in athletics. Not one, in more than a century of Games — in the sports of running, jumping and throwing that every child on Earth plays, the medal table by India’s name said zero. Whole generations of brilliant Indian athletes had come fourth, or close, and gone home.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-neeraj.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.neeraj|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Spear From Khandra","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.neeraj-4":{"src":"story:kh.neeraj:scenes:4:text","title":"The Spear From Khandra · 5 of 6","body":"Nobody could. The spear from Khandra had flown further than the whole world. Neeraj Chopra stood on the top step, the anthem played, and a hundred-and-twenty-year wait ended. In the years after, he became world champion too — and one more lovely thing: his warmest rival, a thrower from Pakistan, became one of his good friends, their two families cheering both boys across the border.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-neeraj.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.neeraj|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Spear From Khandra","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.avani-1":{"src":"story:kh.avani:scenes:1:text","title":"The Stillest Person in the Room · 2 of 5","body":"Her father encouraged her to try sport. She tried archery. Then she tried shooting — the ten-metre air rifle — and something clicked. Around then she read the autobiography of Abhinav Bindra, the shooter who in 2008 had won India’s first-ever individual Olympic gold, and she decided precisely what she was aiming at.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-avani.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.avani|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Stillest Person in the Room","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.avani-3":{"src":"story:kh.avani:scenes:3:text","title":"The Stillest Person in the Room · 4 of 5","body":"Champions are asked one question above all: can you do it again? In Paris, in 2024, with the whole field aiming at her, she came back and won the gold again — beating her own record score. Between the two Games she also quietly got on with studying law. Precision, it seems, is a habit that travels.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-avani.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.avani|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Stillest Person in the Room","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.gukesh-1":{"src":"story:kh.gukesh:scenes:1:text","title":"Eighteen, and King of the World · 2 of 6","body":"He rose absurdly fast. His father, a doctor, set aside his own work to travel with him from tournament to tournament, the family betting years of ordinary life on a boy’s extraordinary chess. At twelve, Gukesh became a grandmaster — at the time, the second-youngest in the history of the game. The academy that helped train him was run by Anand himself: the first king, coaching the next one.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-gukesh.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.gukesh|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Eighteen, and King of the World","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.gukesh-2":{"src":"story:kh.gukesh:scenes:2:text","title":"Eighteen, and King of the World · 3 of 6","body":"In 2024 he won the tournament of champions that decides who challenges for the world title — at seventeen, the youngest ever to do it. That earned him a match against the reigning world champion, Ding Liren of China: fourteen games, in Singapore, stretched across almost a month.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-gukesh.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.gukesh|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Eighteen, and King of the World","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.gukesh-4":{"src":"story:kh.gukesh:scenes:4:text","title":"Eighteen, and King of the World · 5 of 6","body":"A few precise moves later, the world champion resigned — and an eighteen-year-old from Chennai had won the world chess championship, the youngest champion in the long history of the classical game, breaking a record that had stood since before his parents met. The cameras caught him with his head in his hands, crying, a boy and a king at the same time.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-gukesh.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.gukesh|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Eighteen, and King of the World","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.dhoni-1":{"src":"story:kh.dhoni:scenes:1:text","title":"The Ticket Collector Who Finished It · 2 of 6","body":"At twenty he got the kind of job families pray for: a post with Indian Railways, checking tickets at Kharagpur station in Bengal. For two years he lived in the railway quarters, worked the platforms, and played cricket whenever the job allowed. A government job is a warm coat — his family knew what it was worth. And every day, trains full of other people’s journeys pulled out in front of him.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-dhoni.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.dhoni|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Ticket Collector Who Finished It","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.dhoni-2":{"src":"story:kh.dhoni:scenes:2:text","title":"The Ticket Collector Who Finished It · 3 of 6","body":"Within two years the long-haired wicketkeeper from Ranchi was smashing 148 against Pakistan for India.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-dhoni.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.dhoni|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Ticket Collector Who Finished It","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.dhoni-4":{"src":"story:kh.dhoni:scenes:4:text","title":"The Ticket Collector Who Finished It · 5 of 6","body":"The newspapers called him Captain Cool, and the name stuck because it named something real: in the loudest moments cricket can produce, he moved and thought like a man reading a timetable. In 2013 his team added the Champions Trophy — making him the only captain ever to win all three of cricket’s world titles. Calm, it turns out, is not something you have. It is something you practise.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-dhoni.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.dhoni|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Ticket Collector Who Finished It","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.kohli-1":{"src":"story:kh.kohli:scenes:1:text","title":"The Boy Who Batted That Morning · 2 of 6","body":"December 2006. Virat, eighteen, was playing for Delhi in a big match, not out overnight with his team in trouble. In the small hours of that night, at home, his father died suddenly. What happened next is told quietly in Delhi dressing rooms to this day: at dawn the boy telephoned his coach and said he wanted to bat.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-kohli.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.kohli|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Batted That Morning","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.kohli-2":{"src":"story:kh.kohli:scenes:2:text","title":"The Boy Who Batted That Morning · 3 of 6","body":"Cricket had been the language between him and his father, and he kept speaking it. In 2008 he captained India’s under-19s to a World Cup. In 2011, at twenty-two, he was in the team that won the World Cup itself — he is the young man in Sachin’s story on this shelf, one of the teammates who carried the great man around the ground on their shoulders that night.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-kohli.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.kohli|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Batted That Morning","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.kohli-4":{"src":"story:kh.kohli:scenes:4:text","title":"The Boy Who Batted That Morning · 5 of 6","body":"The hundreds kept coming. In November 2023, in Mumbai, he scored his fiftieth one-day international century — passing the record of Sachin Tendulkar, the man he had helped carry, who stood applauding in the stands of his own home ground. The record books hold the number. The photographs hold the passing of something from one pair of hands to another.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-kohli.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.kohli|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Boy Who Batted That Morning","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.mithali-1":{"src":"story:kh.mithali:scenes:1:text","title":"The Dancer at the Crease · 2 of 6","body":"By her early teens, both dance and cricket wanted every morning she had, and she has told the story of the choice many times: it could not be both.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-mithali.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.mithali|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Dancer at the Crease","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.mithali-2":{"src":"story:kh.mithali:scenes:2:text","title":"The Dancer at the Crease · 3 of 6","body":"At sixteen, in 1999, she played her first match for India and scored a century — 114 not out. At nineteen she batted for days against England and made 214, then the highest score in the history of women’s Test cricket. Bowlers changed, decades changed; the calm at the other end of the pitch did not.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-mithali.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.mithali|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Dancer at the Crease","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.mithali-4":{"src":"story:kh.mithali:scenes:4:text","title":"The Dancer at the Crease · 5 of 6","body":"The 2017 final filled Lord’s — the home of cricket, sold out, for a women’s match, with millions more watching across India. England won by nine runs, and it barely mattered to what happened next: girls’ academies filled, the players won contracts, and women’s cricket in India stopped asking for attention because it finally had it.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-mithali.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.mithali|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Dancer at the Crease","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.saina-1":{"src":"story:kh.saina:scenes:1:text","title":"The First Shuttle Through the Door · 2 of 6","body":"When Saina was eight, her scientist father’s work moved the family to Hyderabad — and Hyderabad had real badminton. The academy was on the far side of the city, so her father woke her before daybreak and rode her there on his scooter, tens of kilometres, morning after morning, then waited through practice to ride her back.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-saina.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.saina|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The First Shuttle Through the Door","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.saina-2":{"src":"story:kh.saina:scenes:2:text","title":"The First Shuttle Through the Door · 3 of 6","body":"The mornings compounded. In 2008 she became junior world champion — the first Indian ever. In 2009 she won the Indonesia Open, one of the sport’s biggest crowns, in front of the loudest badminton crowd on Earth — again the first Indian woman to take a title that size. The girl from Hisar was no longer coming up. She had arrived.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-saina.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.saina|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The First Shuttle Through the Door","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.saina-4":{"src":"story:kh.saina:scenes:4:text","title":"The First Shuttle Through the Door · 5 of 6","body":"In April 2015 she reached the top of the world rankings — the first Indian woman ever ranked world number one in badminton. And look at what stands behind her now: Sindhu’s silver and world title came through a door the country had watched Saina hit open. Champions make results; first champions make belief.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-saina.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.saina|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The First Shuttle Through the Door","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.malleswari-1":{"src":"story:kh.malleswari:scenes:1:text","title":"Iron in the Village · 2 of 6","body":"People picture weightlifting as brute force. It is closer to watchmaking done with a hundred kilograms. The two lifts — the snatch, one clean movement from floor to sky, and the clean and jerk, floor to shoulders to sky — are exercises in precision, timing and nerve, where a centimetre of error drops the world on you. Strength gets you into the room. Craft wins.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-malleswari.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.malleswari|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Iron in the Village","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.malleswari-2":{"src":"story:kh.malleswari:scenes:2:text","title":"Iron in the Village · 3 of 6","body":"Malleswari’s craft took her out of the village gym and onto the world stage — and in 1994, in Istanbul, she became champion of the world. In 1995 she did it again. A woman from Srikakulam district, twice world champion in the iron game, years before most of India had ever watched a women’s weightlifting competition.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-malleswari.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.malleswari|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Iron in the Village","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-kh.malleswari-4":{"src":"story:kh.malleswari:scenes:4:text","title":"Iron in the Village · 5 of 6","body":"Now look around this shelf at who came after: Mary Kom’s bronze, Saina’s bronze, Sindhu’s silver, Mirabai’s silver, Avani’s golds. Every one of those champions stepped onto a podium that, for Indian women, Malleswari reached first. That is what a first medal actually buys — not one place in the record books, but a permission slip for a whole country’s daughters.","art":"art/story/sm/kh-malleswari.jpg","route":"#/story/kh.malleswari|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Iron in the Village","facts":[["From","Khel — India at Play"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-jcbose-1":{"src":"story:sci-jcbose:scenes:1:text","title":"The Scientist Who Listened to Plants · 2 of 6","body":"But the question that would not let go of him was about plants. A dog runs, a person cries out — how would a plant, which can do neither, ever tell you anything? Bose's answer: build it a way to be heard. His crescograph magnified a plant's growth many thousands of times, so that growth too slow for any eye became a moving line you could watch — a plant, growing, live.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-jcbose.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-jcbose|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Scientist Who Listened to Plants","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-jcbose-2":{"src":"story:sci-jcbose:scenes:2:text","title":"The Scientist Who Listened to Plants · 3 of 6","body":"And the line had news in it. Chill the plant, and the line slowed. Touch it roughly, give it something nasty to drink, and the line stumbled and flinched. The plant was responding — not sitting there at all, but reacting to its world, in its own silent, slow-motion way. Bose travelled to the great scientific societies of Europe and showed them, with the instruments he had built in Kolkata.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-jcbose.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-jcbose|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Scientist Who Listened to Plants","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-jcbose-4":{"src":"story:sci-jcbose:scenes:4:text","title":"The Scientist Who Listened to Plants · 5 of 6","body":"In 1917 he opened the Bose Institute in Kolkata — one of the first research institutes in Asia — and gave his instruments, his methods and his life's work to it, for whoever came next. It is still there, still working.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-jcbose.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-jcbose|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Scientist Who Listened to Plants","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-raman-1":{"src":"story:sci-raman:scenes:1:text","title":"Why Is the Sea Blue? · 2 of 6","body":"He had a small optical device in his luggage — he was the kind of traveller who would — and it could filter out reflected light. He pointed it at the sea, cutting away the sky's reflection. If the books were right, the blue should vanish with it. The blue stayed. The sea was not borrowing its colour from the sky. The water itself was scattering sunlight and making its own blue.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-raman.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-raman|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why Is the Sea Blue?","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-raman-2":{"src":"story:sci-raman:scenes:2:text","title":"Why Is the Sea Blue? · 3 of 6","body":"A colour is not just a fact — it is a message from inside the water. Back at his laboratory in Kolkata, the Indian Association for the Cultivation of Science, Raman and his colleagues, above all K. S. Krishnan, spent seven years shining light through liquids and reading what came out.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-raman.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-raman|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why Is the Sea Blue?","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-raman-4":{"src":"story:sci-raman:scenes:4:text","title":"Why Is the Sea Blue? · 5 of 6","body":"In 1930 Raman won the Nobel Prize in Physics — the first person from Asia to win a science Nobel. His biographers tell that he was so sure it was coming, he booked his tickets to Stockholm months before the announcement. That is either tremendous confidence or tremendous cheek, and with Raman it was generally both.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-raman.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-raman|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from Why Is the Sea Blue?","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-ramanujan-1":{"src":"story:sci-ramanujan:scenes:1:text","title":"The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge · 2 of 6","body":"In January 1913 he did a brave and slightly desperate thing: he wrote to G. H. Hardy, one of the most famous mathematicians in England — a man he had never met, in a country he had never seen. He introduced himself honestly as a clerk with no university degree, and enclosed page after page of theorems, more than a hundred of them, with no proofs.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-ramanujan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-ramanujan|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-sci-ramanujan-2":{"src":"story:sci-ramanujan:scenes:2:text","title":"The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge · 3 of 6","body":"Some results Hardy recognised. Some were subtly wrong. And some were like nothing he had ever seen — Hardy wrote afterwards that such theorems had to be true, because no one would have had the imagination to invent them. He arranged to bring their author to Cambridge, and for five years the clerk and the professor did mathematics together that people are still mining today.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-ramanujan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-ramanujan|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-sci-ramanujan-4":{"src":"story:sci-ramanujan:scenes:4:text","title":"The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge · 5 of 6","body":"England's cold and the war years were hard on him, and he was often ill. He sailed home to India in 1919, and died the next year, at just thirty-two. But he worked to the very end, and the notebooks he left — including one found decades later — are still yielding new mathematics today. Mathematicians speak of him the way musicians speak of Mozart.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-ramanujan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-ramanujan|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Clerk Who Wrote to Cambridge","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","5 min"]]},"ss-sci-janaki-1":{"src":"story:sci-janaki:scenes:1:text","title":"The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane · 2 of 6","body":"She came home Dr Janaki Ammal — remembered as the first Indian woman to earn a doctorate in botany. Her science was cytogenetics: counting and reading chromosomes, the tiny thread-libraries inside every living cell that decide what a plant can be. Counting chromosomes sounds humble. It is actually a master key — it tells you which plants can cross with which, and what their children might become.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-janaki|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-janaki-2":{"src":"story:sci-janaki:scenes:2:text","title":"The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane · 3 of 6","body":"And India had a problem worth a master key. At the Sugarcane Breeding Institute in Coimbatore she faced it: India's own canes were tough — they could take India's droughts and soils — but thin in sweetness, so the sweetest planting cane came from Java, abroad. Tough but not sweet; sweet but not tough.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-janaki|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-janaki-4":{"src":"story:sci-janaki:scenes:4:text","title":"The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane · 5 of 6","body":"India asked her back, to reorganise the Botanical Survey of India — the great national accounting of every plant the land holds. And in her eighties she took up one more fight: scientists were campaigning to save Silent Valley, an ancient rainforest in Kerala, from being dammed and drowned. She joined them, studied it, spoke for it. The forest stands today, a national park, full of birdsong.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-janaki.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-janaki|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Woman Who Sweetened the Sugarcane","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-bhabha-1":{"src":"story:sci-bhabha:scenes:1:text","title":"A Home for Science · 2 of 6","body":"He took a post at the Indian Institute of Science in Bangalore, in C. V. Raman's orbit, and looked hard at a fact he had always known: a young Indian who wanted to do the world's best physics had to leave India to do it. Not for lack of minds — for lack of a home. No buildings, no instruments, no salaries, no colleagues down the corridor. Science, he understood, is not a lone genius in a shed.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-bhabha|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from A Home for Science","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-bhabha-2":{"src":"story:sci-bhabha:scenes:2:text","title":"A Home for Science · 3 of 6","body":"So in 1944 — before India was even independent — he sat down and wrote a letter to the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust, proposing an institute for fundamental research. The letter survives, and one sentence in it has become famous: when nuclear energy was successfully used for power, he wrote, India would not have to look abroad for its experts, but would find them ready at hand.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-bhabha|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from A Home for Science","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-bhabha-4":{"src":"story:sci-bhabha:scenes:4:text","title":"A Home for Science · 5 of 6","body":"And the famous sentence came true on schedule. When India came to build its atomic programme, it did not have to look abroad — the scientists were ready at hand, trained in the house that Bhabha built. TIFR stands by the sea today, still doing mathematics and physics at the world's edge, gardens and paintings and all.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-bhabha.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-bhabha|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from A Home for Science","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-swaminathan-1":{"src":"story:sci-swaminathan:scenes:1:text","title":"The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over · 2 of 5","body":"By the 1960s India was importing wheat by the shipload and eating it almost off the docks — people called it living ship-to-mouth. Here was the maddening trap: give Indian wheat plenty of water and fertiliser and it grew tall, top-heavy — and then fell flat in the wind and rotted in the mud. Feed it well and you flattened it.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-swaminathan-3":{"src":"story:sci-swaminathan:scenes:3:text","title":"The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over · 4 of 5","body":"And one honest sentence, because this story has earned it: the new farming drank deep — heavy water, heavy fertiliser — and in time the land began presenting its bills, in tired soils and falling wells. Swaminathan said so himself, and spent his later decades on what he called an evergreen revolution — his own phrase — meaning harvests that stay high without wearing out the earth that gives them.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-swaminathan.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-swaminathan|s3","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Wheat That Would Not Fall Over","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-salimali-1":{"src":"story:sci-salimali:scenes:1:text","title":"The Fall of a Sparrow · 2 of 6","body":"He could have shrugged. Instead he carried the bird to his uncle, who did an unusually wise thing for a busy uncle: he sent the boy with a note to the Bombay Natural History Society. There, a courteous Englishman named W. S. Millard did not laugh at a small boy with a dead sparrow. He opened drawer after drawer of carefully kept bird skins until they found the match: a yellow-throated sparrow.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-salimali|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Fall of a Sparrow","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-salimali-2":{"src":"story:sci-salimali:scenes:2:text","title":"The Fall of a Sparrow · 3 of 6","body":"The boy who had shot birds became the man who counted them. For decade after decade Salim Ali walked India doing bird surveys — princely state by princely state, forest by marsh by mountain — with notebooks, patience and famously little money, finding out for the first time exactly which birds lived where in India.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-salimali|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Fall of a Sparrow","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-salimali-4":{"src":"story:sci-salimali:scenes:4:text","title":"The Fall of a Sparrow · 5 of 6","body":"When he finally wrote his life down, he named the book after the bird that began it: The Fall of a Sparrow. He never pretended the beginning was tidy — a shot bird, a boy's bad aim, a stab of curiosity. What he did with the next seventy years is the part he offered as the lesson.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-salimali.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-salimali|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Fall of a Sparrow","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-annamani-1":{"src":"story:sci-annamani:scenes:1:text","title":"The Girl Who Refused the Earrings · 2 of 6","body":"The reading girl became a physics student in Madras, and then a researcher in the laboratory of C. V. Raman himself — you have met him earlier on this shelf — where she spent years teasing out the secrets of light inside diamonds and rubies. She wrote five research papers of real quality.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-annamani.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-annamani|s1","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-annamani-2":{"src":"story:sci-annamani:scenes:2:text","title":"The Girl Who Refused the Earrings · 3 of 6","body":"In 1948, after training in instrument-making in England, she joined the Meteorological Department of newly independent India — and found a quiet embarrassment: nearly every instrument India used to read its own weather, every barometer and thermometer and rain gauge, was imported. A country whose farms, ships and monsoon forecasts hung on the weather could not build the tools that measured it.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-annamani.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-annamani|s2","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"ss-sci-annamani-4":{"src":"story:sci-annamani:scenes:4:text","title":"The Girl Who Refused the Earrings · 5 of 6","body":"She kept going where the air got thinner. She built and flew instruments to measure ozone — the fragile, invisible layer that shields all life from the sun's harshest rays — years before the world grew properly worried about it, and served on the international commission that watches it.","art":"art/story/sm/sci-annamani.jpg","route":"#/story/sci-annamani|s4","cta":"Read from here","why":"A moment from The Girl Who Refused the Earrings","facts":[["From","Vigyan — The Scientists"],["Read","4 min"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"His grandfather. The teacher who had taught him to hold a bow. Kripa, who had taught him before that. Cousins he had grown up with. Boys he had wrestled. Men whose houses he had eaten in.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"And his hands started shaking, and he sat down in the chariot, and put the bow on the floor of it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"\"That is my grandfather,\" he said. \"That is the man who put this bow in my hands and told me to look at the eye. What exactly am I supposed to win here? A kingdom I have to sit and eat in, with all their families at the table?\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"\"I would rather they killed me. I mean that. Let them come. I am not going to do this.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"And what happens next is not a general shouting at a soldier. It is one friend talking to another friend, in the middle of a field, with two armies standing still, about the hardest question there is: what do you do when every single thing you can do is bad?","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-7":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:7:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"He talked about doing the work in front of you, and doing it as well as you can, and then letting go of owning what comes of it — because nobody, ever, owns what comes of it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"He talked about the part of a person that a weapon cannot get at. He talked about doing your own job badly rather than somebody else's job beautifully. And he told him, at the end, to think about all of it and then decide for himself — which is not the sentence anybody expects.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"And somewhere in that chariot something happened that Arjuna could never afterwards describe to anybody, and he was frightened, and he asked for his friend back, and got him.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"That conversation is the Bhagavad Gita. Seven hundred verses, sitting quietly inside a war book, before a single arrow is shot. What you have just read is the shape of it, in our words — the verses themselves are in Sanskrit and you will find them on the verse shelf, read out one at a time by a person. Nobody should ever hand you a made-up version of those.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-26-11":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|26","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":26},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:25:cards:11:text","title":"The Mahabharata · A Talk Between Friends","body":"People have argued about this conversation for a very long time, including about whether it settles anything at all. Gandhi read the whole battlefield as a picture of a fight going on inside one person. Other readers have always taken it as exactly what it looks like. It has carried both for centuries.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 26","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","26 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-1":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:1:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"A dog joined them somewhere on the road out. Nobody invited it and nobody sent it away. It simply fell in beside them and kept up, the way dogs do.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-2":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:2:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"They went up into the mountains, and up past the snow, and one by one they fell on the way. Draupadi first. Then Sahadeva, then Nakula, then Arjuna, then Bhima. Each time, Yudhishthira kept walking and did not look back, because he had been told that this was the road and you do not stop on it.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-3":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:3:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"The dog kept up. It did not fall. It walked beside him the whole way to the top, with its head down, in the cold.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-4":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:4:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"At the top there was a chariot waiting, and Indra himself standing in it, and the whole sky lit up behind him. \"Get in,\" he said. \"You walked the road. You are the only one who did. It is all yours.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-5":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:5:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"\"Thank you,\" said Yudhishthira. \"Wait a moment while I lift the dog in.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-6":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:6:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"\"The dog does not come,\" said Indra. \"There are no dogs up there. It is not that sort of place.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-8":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:8:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"\"You would give up all of it? For a dog?\" \"I would give up all of it for anything that trusted me,\" he said. \"There is no version of this where I get in without him. Drive on if you like.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-9":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:9:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"And the dog stood up, and it was Dharma — his own father, the same one who had been in the lake all those years ago with a voice and a set of riddles, back for one last question at one last doorway.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-10":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:10:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"It was not over even then. He went up and found Duryodhana sitting comfortably in heaven and his own brothers nowhere in sight, and was told where they were, and turned round without a word and went to them and said he would stay there instead. \"If they are here, I am here.\" And then that lifted too, because it had been the last test, and everybody was where they should be.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-11":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:11:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"\"So it ends where it began,\" said Mithu. \"With a question at a doorway and a man who answers it honestly and loses everything he wanted, and it turns out that is the answer.\"","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]},"en-mahabharata-33-12":{"route":"#/epic/mahabharata|33","act":{"a":"episode","id":"mahabharata","n":33},"src":"epic:mahabharata:episodes:32:cards:12:text","title":"The Mahabharata · The Dog at the Gate","body":"That is the Mahabharata. It has taken you thirty-three sittings and it took the poet a lifetime, and there are whole books of it we have not touched. There are Mahabharatas in Tamil and Telugu and Odia and Bengali where these people say different things, and village versions where Draupadi is a goddess with her own temple, and Karna is the hero, and Duryodhana has one too.","art":null,"cta":"Hear this night","why":"The Mahabharata, night 33","facts":[["Epic","The Mahabharata"],["Night","33 of 33"]]}});
